WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.994 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.994 --> 00:00:04.491 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:07.485 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.485 --> 00:00:16.280 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.590 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: So today,
we'll be talking

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:24.200 align:middle line:84%
about portfolio management as
one of the application lectures.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.000 align:middle line:84%
So I will share some
of my research work

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.960 align:middle line:84%
from a practitioner's
point of view,

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.450 align:middle line:84%
rather than from pure
theoretical or math

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.530 align:middle line:90%
point of view.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:40.490 align:middle line:84%
So as we mentioned at the
beginning of the term,

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:45.090 align:middle line:84%
we've been teaching
this class since 2012.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:52.310 align:middle line:84%
And in 2013, this class was
recorded for MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.740 align:middle line:90%
The link, I put it here.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.400 align:middle line:90%
It's the lecture from 2013.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.030 align:middle line:84%
Some of you may have
watched that lecture.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:06.230 align:middle line:84%
It actually turned out to be one
of the top most viewed courses.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.290 align:middle line:84%
Our course is one of
the top five, actually,

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:10.450 align:middle line:90%
on the OpenCourseWare.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.420 align:middle line:84%
So I want to promise
you, in today's lecture,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.680 align:middle line:84%
I will give you a
lot of new content.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:24.950 align:middle line:84%
So you will make showing up,
your presence here, worthwhile.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.530 align:middle line:84%
So we can also make
it interactive.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:34.270 align:middle line:84%
So this is the outline of what
I will be talking about today.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.680 align:middle line:84%
So first, I'll talk about
what really all this portfolio

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.170 align:middle line:84%
construction is about, coming
from a very basic level.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.680 align:middle line:84%
Then we'll talk about what
is the endowment model.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.970 align:middle line:84%
How do we manage
the endowment fund?

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.520 align:middle line:84%
Then I'll talk about
the portfolio theory

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.260 align:middle line:84%
and illustrate it in some really
special and simple cases for you

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.180 align:middle line:90%
to understand the math.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.790 align:middle line:84%
Then I will really switch gears
and talk about the limitations

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.290 align:middle line:90%
of this theory.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.150 align:middle line:84%
The theory was
proposed in the 1950s.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.419 align:middle line:84%
I mean, of course,
a lot of people

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:15.340 align:middle line:84%
have worked in the field, tried
to overcome the shortcomings

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.580 align:middle line:90%
of these theories.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.710 align:middle line:84%
So I myself have been
spending a lot of time

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.520 align:middle line:90%
thinking about these problems.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.410 align:middle line:84%
So I will talk about
my own research work,

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.770 align:middle line:84%
in terms of how to improve the
portfolio construction theory--

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:35.050 align:middle line:84%
for example, how to improve
the risk measurement using

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:41.540 align:middle line:84%
volatility and Sharpe ratio and
how to model crowding behavior.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.670 align:middle line:84%
And also, you probably
heard the power law,

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:50.770 align:middle line:84%
how this behavior actually
originated from the crowding

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:51.680 align:middle line:90%
behavior.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.460 align:middle line:90%
Now, I will summarize.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.900 align:middle line:84%
So before we start
the lecture, I

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.160 align:middle line:84%
want to do an exercise
with all of you.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to hand over a
piece of paper to all of you.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.310 align:middle line:90%
Please pass it down.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.240 align:middle line:84%
Make sure everyone gets
one piece of paper.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.940 align:middle line:84%
Now, what I want you to do is to
write down your own investment

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.320 align:middle line:90%
portfolio.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:23.280 align:middle line:84%
Just hypothetically, think about
you have $10,000 to manage.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.050 align:middle line:84%
And put down investments you'd
like to have in the portfolio.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.250 align:middle line:84%
Put down the percentage
of each investment.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:38.130 align:middle line:84%
And I also want you not to
think too hard and also too long

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:38.920 align:middle line:90%
on this.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.750 align:middle line:90%
Just use your intuition.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.770 align:middle line:84%
Imagine you're now a
fund manager sitting

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.090 align:middle line:84%
at a pension fund, or
endowment fund, or hedge fund.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.850 align:middle line:84%
I'm just asking you to
pick a few investments.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:56.370 align:middle line:84%
And then just hand it back
to me after you finish.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.260 align:middle line:84%
Then we will talk about,
from where you started, how

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.460 align:middle line:90%
to do the portfolio management.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.020 align:middle line:90%


00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.220 align:middle line:84%
So just spend a
couple of minutes.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.840 align:middle line:90%
Don't over spend your time.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.350 align:middle line:84%
So while I'm talking, so you
can write down your choices.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.850 align:middle line:84%
So when you received that
blank sheet of paper,

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.060 align:middle line:84%
you must be thinking, what are
the criteria for me to do this?

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.060 align:middle line:90%
What are the considerations?

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:30.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is exactly how I
want to lead you into how

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.517 align:middle line:90%
to think about these problems.

00:04:32.517 --> 00:04:34.100 align:middle line:84%
First of all, you
need to think about,

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.690 align:middle line:84%
what's the objective
of doing this exercise?

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.400 align:middle line:84%
So always say, oh, I want
to get a great return

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.520 align:middle line:90%
for the investment.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.200 align:middle line:90%
Sure.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.550 align:middle line:90%
So you have a return objective.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.440 align:middle line:84%
But is that what is
the great return?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.820 align:middle line:90%
Is that 10%, 50%, or 100%?

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:54.640 align:middle line:84%
What is the time horizon
we are talking about?

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.710 align:middle line:84%
And because the investment
is highly uncertain,

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.970 align:middle line:90%
what's your loss tolerance?

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.130 align:middle line:90%
You think about it.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:05.030 align:middle line:90%
You have a $10,000.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.480 align:middle line:90%
How much can you afford to lose?

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.890 align:middle line:90%
So those are the questions.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.060 align:middle line:84%
Then when you pick
those investments,

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:17.170 align:middle line:84%
you should also ask
yourself, what's

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.790 align:middle line:84%
your own ability of
picking a winner?

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:24.850 align:middle line:84%
Why do you think you have an
edge in predicting that market

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.130 align:middle line:90%
or investment?

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:29.320 align:middle line:84%
Then of course, how
many investments

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.970 align:middle line:84%
you should have
in the portfolio?

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:36.340 align:middle line:90%
Is that 1, 5, or 50, or 100?

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.170 align:middle line:84%
How diversified
or how well-mixed

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:45.130 align:middle line:84%
that investment
portfolio should be?

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:48.130 align:middle line:84%
Then ultimately, the
most important question

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:52.920 align:middle line:84%
is, how do you your size
each of the investments?

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.870 align:middle line:90%
It comes all down to sizing.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.770 align:middle line:84%
So portfolio construction,
I will show you later,

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.270 align:middle line:84%
is really about, when you
have a list of investments

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.720 align:middle line:84%
you like, how do you
size them accordingly

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.670 align:middle line:84%
in relative amounts to each
other, to the whole portfolio?

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:18.360 align:middle line:84%
So now, please hand
it back because I

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.360 align:middle line:84%
want you to listen to me,
not just spending the time

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.860 align:middle line:90%
on writing down your portfolio.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.374 align:middle line:90%


00:06:27.374 --> 00:06:29.335 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Do we put
our names on it?

00:06:29.335 --> 00:06:30.460 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: It's your choice.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:31.870 align:middle line:90%
Name or no name is fine.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.090 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I'm not going
to call your names.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.110 align:middle line:84%
But I will probably use
some of your answers

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:43.110 align:middle line:84%
as examples to talk
about investments.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:52.430 align:middle line:84%
So as we are collecting
these portfolio choices,

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:59.150 align:middle line:84%
I'm also going to talk about, in
your decision making process--

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.550 align:middle line:84%
so when you choose
investments, you probably

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:03.870 align:middle line:84%
were thinking
about what markets,

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:08.510 align:middle line:84%
first of all, you will be
selecting your investments from.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.876 align:middle line:90%
And what are the instruments?

00:07:11.876 --> 00:07:17.435 align:middle line:90%


00:07:17.435 --> 00:07:20.240 align:middle line:90%
Are we mostly finished?

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.520 align:middle line:84%
So by the way, for
you, we are doing

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.240 align:middle line:84%
an exercise, where we ask you
to put down your investment

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:27.060 align:middle line:90%
portfolio.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.250 align:middle line:84%
Just put down the percentage,
whatever you can do.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:33.097 align:middle line:90%
So anyway--

00:07:33.097 --> 00:07:33.680 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Here.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:34.530 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: You're done?

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:35.030 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:38.840 align:middle line:90%


00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.220 align:middle line:84%
For the rest of you, if you
want to take a longer time,

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:41.970 align:middle line:90%
that's fine.

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:43.890 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to use
some of the examples.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:49.550 align:middle line:84%
So the decision making process
I was talking about-- you

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.610 align:middle line:84%
need to decide which market
to pick your investment.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:55.550 align:middle line:84%
Then you need to think
about what data to collect,

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:58.230 align:middle line:84%
what kind of signals
you can derive,

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.360 align:middle line:84%
and what models
you need to build,

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:04.130 align:middle line:84%
what factors you need to
take into consideration, what

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.710 align:middle line:84%
predictions you
need to make, then

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.300 align:middle line:90%
assemble them into strategies.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.130 align:middle line:84%
Then we're talking about sizing
and allocating your capital

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.110 align:middle line:84%
portfolio construction,
then optimize it,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.610 align:middle line:84%
then at the end really
to manage the risk.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:25.130 align:middle line:84%
So now, you can see what
we really come down to is

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:28.110 align:middle line:84%
how to compare
different investments.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:32.210 align:middle line:84%
To understand the relationship
between different investments

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:36.110 align:middle line:84%
is to understand the return
and the risk profiles of all

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.400 align:middle line:90%
the investments.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.270 align:middle line:90%
So what is risk?

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.360 align:middle line:84%
So in this chart we
are talking about here,

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:45.440 align:middle line:84%
I'm using volatility
for the time being,

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.710 align:middle line:84%
which represents
uncertainty of the outcome.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:55.810 align:middle line:84%
Later on, I will come
back and tell you

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:59.650 align:middle line:84%
volatility is not really
the best measure for risk.

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:02.090 align:middle line:90%
But let's save that for later.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.810 align:middle line:84%
But before we talk about
the investment choices,

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:09.940 align:middle line:84%
let me look at some of
what you have written down.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:18.980 align:middle line:84%
So one portfolio here has
half in S&P straddle--

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.050 align:middle line:84%
so this is actually
option strategy--

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:26.530 align:middle line:84%
and then 30% in
VIX related ETFs,

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:30.890 align:middle line:90%
and 20% in dollar index futures.

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:35.860 align:middle line:84%
So it sounds like someone has
done an internship somewhere

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.426 align:middle line:90%
knowing option strategies.

00:09:37.426 --> 00:09:39.940 align:middle line:90%


00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:45.180 align:middle line:84%
So this portfolio has
about eight investments.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:50.820 align:middle line:84%
The largest one is
cash, 20%, and 20%

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.773 align:middle line:90%
in QQQ, which is NASDAQ ETF.

00:09:53.773 --> 00:09:57.180 align:middle line:90%


00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.670 align:middle line:90%
Let me see.

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.330 align:middle line:84%
This one is 70%
bonds, 30% stocks.

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:08.490 align:middle line:84%
This one is quite interesting
because, when we normally talk

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:16.230 align:middle line:84%
about 60/40 or 70/30 portfolios,
the bigger piece tends to be

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:17.550 align:middle line:90%
the equity piece.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:24.090 align:middle line:84%
But this one probably has more
risk neutral or risk parity type

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.275 align:middle line:90%
of construction.

00:10:25.275 --> 00:10:26.150 align:middle line:90%
So we'll get to that.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.800 align:middle line:90%


00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.110 align:middle line:84%
This has many
single name stocks,

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.610 align:middle line:90%
Microsoft being on the top.

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:43.700 align:middle line:84%
And this is a 50% S&P,
50% Vanguard Pell fund.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.720 align:middle line:90%
Probably that's what is written.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.870 align:middle line:84%
And this 100% S&P.
Yeah, very much

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:53.030 align:middle line:90%
representing the average market.

00:10:53.030 --> 00:11:02.250 align:middle line:84%
And so this one probably
has five stocks, 20% cash.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:10.560 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, 50% US Treasury bonds,
25% S&P. So fairly similar.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.160 align:middle line:90%
So now, you've got the idea.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:18.050 align:middle line:84%
And Peter and I used to
review, over the years,

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:21.210 align:middle line:84%
year to year change of
the investment selection.

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:26.690 align:middle line:84%
Only three years ago, in
2021, most of the class

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:31.250 align:middle line:90%
chose some cryptocurrencies.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:35.850 align:middle line:84%
And Dogecoin's being the top
one being selected by everyone.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:39.830 align:middle line:84%
I haven't seen any
crypto investments today.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:43.210 align:middle line:84%
So that just tells you
the index investing

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:47.152 align:middle line:84%
ETF seems to be the
theme of this year.

00:11:47.152 --> 00:11:48.170 align:middle line:90%
I don't blame you.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.060 align:middle line:90%
I think it's a pretty good idea.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:55.330 align:middle line:84%
S&P, if you don't have
particular edge you

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:57.940 align:middle line:84%
feel you like to have,
Investing in index

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.160 align:middle line:90%
is a pretty good choice.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.130 align:middle line:84%
So on this graph,
I'm showing you

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:06.050 align:middle line:90%
here return versus volatility.

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:11.020 align:middle line:84%
So we already touched upon--
a lot of people chose cash.

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:15.040 align:middle line:84%
Cash, basically, you can
view it as 0 volatility,

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.720 align:middle line:90%
as some minimum return.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.960 align:middle line:90%
The Fed just cut interest rate.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:26.750 align:middle line:84%
It's still over 4%, close
to 5% And they may cut more.

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:29.500 align:middle line:84%
But compared to
the interest rate

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:31.850 align:middle line:84%
where it has been in
the last 10 years,

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:36.220 align:middle line:84%
this is actually not a bad
place to park your capital.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:39.760 align:middle line:84%
Bonds have slightly higher
return, a little more

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.000 align:middle line:90%
uncertainty of return.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:47.230 align:middle line:84%
Stocks definitely have more, but
the index has less volatility.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:51.130 align:middle line:84%
And the index, return
wise, it may not

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:55.500 align:middle line:84%
be lagging the
broad stock market.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:58.150 align:middle line:84%
I mean, as you
know, the index now

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:03.620 align:middle line:84%
is heavily weighted towards
large cap tech stocks.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.760 align:middle line:84%
And lately, those
large cap tech stocks

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:08.750 align:middle line:90%
have been doing really well.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:12.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you're long
QQQ or S&P, you're

00:13:12.280 --> 00:13:15.560 align:middle line:84%
essentially long the
Magnificent Seven stocks.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:19.690 align:middle line:84%
And so the ETF
investment choice here

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:23.170 align:middle line:84%
is not a bad choice
or index investment.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:27.850 align:middle line:84%
Further up on the right is
the private equity and venture

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:32.270 align:middle line:84%
capital choice, which I haven't
seen anyone put it down.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:37.960 align:middle line:84%
So you may be more thinking
about the public markets.

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.320 align:middle line:84%
But private equity
and venture capital

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:44.250 align:middle line:84%
are basically investing in
private companies not publicly

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.390 align:middle line:90%
listed on stock exchanges.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:49.510 align:middle line:84%
So those companies
tend to be illiquid.

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:51.760 align:middle line:84%
You cannot get your
money out easily.

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.850 align:middle line:84%
You have to stay with
them for a very long time.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:58.170 align:middle line:84%
But a lot of times,
you have more influence

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:02.940 align:middle line:84%
on the company's
operation and venture.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.460 align:middle line:84%
For example, you invest
in start-up companies.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:13.260 align:middle line:84%
Those companies can grow very
fast, can also fail very fast.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:17.410 align:middle line:84%
So the outcome, the
uncertainty range, is very big.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.130 align:middle line:84%
But overall, over
the years they tend

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:25.800 align:middle line:84%
to produce higher returns to
justify the uncertainty or risk

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:30.660 align:middle line:90%
premium you are dealing with.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:36.020 align:middle line:84%
So coming back to the return
objective and loss tolerance,

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:40.290 align:middle line:84%
we should think about when
you write down your portfolio.

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:43.410 align:middle line:84%
So you try to maximize
your return for sure.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:48.500 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone here disagree with
you should maximize your return?

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:49.350 align:middle line:90%
Probably not.

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:53.520 align:middle line:84%
At the same time, you
should minimize your risk.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:56.370 align:middle line:84%
In this case, minimize
your uncertainty.

00:14:56.370 --> 00:15:00.990 align:middle line:84%
So you want to maximize the
ratio of return over volatility.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.350 align:middle line:90%
That's one of the objectives.

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:06.420 align:middle line:84%
But you may have
constraints as well.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:08.900 align:middle line:84%
There are certain
investments you may not

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:12.110 align:middle line:90%
want to participate in.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:16.760 align:middle line:84%
For example, many
universities declared

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:19.230 align:middle line:84%
we don't want to
invest in fossil fuels

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:21.540 align:middle line:90%
for the environmental concerns.

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.740 align:middle line:84%
And in the past, like
tobacco companies

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:28.010 align:middle line:84%
or some kind of
weapon companies,

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:33.940 align:middle line:84%
people have, in principle,
reasons to go against them.

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:38.740 align:middle line:84%
And so there are constraints
of what your investment

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:40.780 align:middle line:90%
universe should be.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:46.280 align:middle line:84%
Then there's liquidity, how
much you need to use the money.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:50.590 align:middle line:84%
The $10,000 you had
in the portfolio,

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:54.670 align:middle line:84%
you may not want to use it all
in the investment or illiquid

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:56.030 align:middle line:90%
investment all the time.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:00.500 align:middle line:84%
So you may want to spend
5% a year, for example.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:05.200 align:middle line:84%
So you need to take into
account your spending needs.

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:10.940 align:middle line:84%
And also on the down side, how
much you can afford to lose

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:12.800 align:middle line:84%
is really a very
important question.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:16.740 align:middle line:84%
So if your $10,000
becomes $5,000,

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.860 align:middle line:90%
will you feel really bad?

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:24.250 align:middle line:84%
You would just say, I'm going
to stop investing altogether.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:25.930 align:middle line:90%
I cannot afford to lose.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:30.507 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's the tuition I saved
for next year, I put aside.

00:16:30.507 --> 00:16:31.840 align:middle line:90%
I just want to earn some return.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:32.840 align:middle line:90%
I don't want to lose it.

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:37.420 align:middle line:84%
So then your risk
tolerance is very low.

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:39.390 align:middle line:90%
It's very different.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:42.990 align:middle line:84%
So for many asset
owners, inflation

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:47.400 align:middle line:84%
is also an important concern
because wealth represents

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:50.340 align:middle line:90%
relative purchasing power.

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:53.610 align:middle line:84%
So how much wealth
you have is not really

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:56.520 align:middle line:84%
just the dollar amount you
have in your bank account.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:59.850 align:middle line:90%
It's how much you can buy.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:03.700 align:middle line:84%
So when the price of goods
and services are going up,

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:06.119 align:middle line:84%
your purchasing
power is going down.

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:07.569 align:middle line:90%
So that's inflation.

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:12.930 align:middle line:84%
And it's also relative to the
next group of people or person,

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:14.319 align:middle line:90%
how much they can buy.

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:19.599 align:middle line:84%
So for universities, that means
you want to hire a professor.

00:17:19.599 --> 00:17:22.980 align:middle line:84%
If there are competing
offers, how much more

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:27.780 align:middle line:84%
you can offer to the
professor to get them

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:30.930 align:middle line:90%
to come to your university.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:35.700 align:middle line:84%
So there's definitely absolute
return angle for the requirement

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.680 align:middle line:90%
of the portfolio.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:40.530 align:middle line:84%
And there's also a
lot of people nowadays

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:44.880 align:middle line:84%
talk about beating a
benchmark, beating an index.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.760 align:middle line:84%
Many of you have
chosen S&P as an index.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:53.790 align:middle line:84%
But if you can produce more
return on top of S&P return,

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:57.210 align:middle line:84%
that's called the
alpha, relative return.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:18:01.050 align:middle line:84%
That's also often being used
as a measurement of investment

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:01.990 align:middle line:90%
skills.

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:06.640 align:middle line:84%
So you don't just put in a beta
or broad market investment.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:10.500 align:middle line:84%
You actually can
extract more return.

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:15.880 align:middle line:84%
So asset liability matching,
cash flow is very important.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:20.940 align:middle line:84%
So 2008 taught many asset
owners a big lesson.

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:25.490 align:middle line:84%
When you don't have the
right asset liability

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:29.280 align:middle line:84%
match, when your
portfolio has a drawdown,

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:32.180 align:middle line:84%
your liability
side will basically

00:18:32.180 --> 00:18:34.800 align:middle line:90%
dominate on your balance sheet.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:38.270 align:middle line:84%
You need to cut your
spending budget,

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:42.810 align:middle line:84%
stop your building projects,
and lay off people.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:46.460 align:middle line:90%
So those are painful lessons.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.170 align:middle line:84%
You need to have the
cash flow well planned.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.750 align:middle line:84%
So time horizon, I
mentioned, is very important.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:53.280 align:middle line:84%
When I gave you
that piece of paper,

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:56.700 align:middle line:84%
I didn't say time
horizon on purpose.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:57.980 align:middle line:90%
I want you to think about it.

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:01.730 align:middle line:84%
You should ask the question--
this portfolio is for how long?

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:06.810 align:middle line:84%
Is that for one day, or for
a year, or for 10 years?

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:13.730 align:middle line:84%
So in the investment world,
people always compare returns,

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:17.990 align:middle line:84%
like year to year, sometimes
even quarter to quarter.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:20.900 align:middle line:84%
Peers try to say,
oh, I outperformed--

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:23.930 align:middle line:84%
A outperformed B.
So therefore, A

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:27.460 align:middle line:84%
is better than B.
So this is actually,

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:32.320 align:middle line:84%
a lot of times, unhealthy
exercise because, for long term

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:35.600 align:middle line:84%
investors, you should really
focus on long term return.

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:38.330 align:middle line:84%
But inevitably,
in the real world,

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:41.030 align:middle line:84%
people look at
short term returns.

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:42.830 align:middle line:90%
So you cannot ignore it.

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:47.570 align:middle line:84%
But that's the peer pressure for
a lot of investment managers.

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:50.600 align:middle line:84%
That's the career risk
because their board may think,

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.190 align:middle line:84%
in the short term
they are not good.

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:55.330 align:middle line:90%
They try to replace them.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:20:03.790 align:middle line:84%
So coming back more on why
investing, so on the left,

00:20:03.790 --> 00:20:08.650 align:middle line:84%
on your left, if you look
at the personal income

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:15.940 align:middle line:84%
and spending as one person's
age, as age as the function,

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:19.930 align:middle line:84%
so your income
level kind of peaks

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:23.704 align:middle line:90%
somewhere in your mid-career.

00:20:23.704 --> 00:20:27.070 align:middle line:84%
So let's call it
around the age of 50.

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.430 align:middle line:90%
So you're earning the most.

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:31.780 align:middle line:84%
You're probably also
spending the most.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:36.070 align:middle line:84%
But you as a student, you
probably don't earn that much.

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:38.830 align:middle line:84%
You don't spend
that much either.

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:43.840 align:middle line:84%
I mean, same is true when
you're over the peak point.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:46.420 align:middle line:84%
You're getting into
the retirement stage.

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:47.860 align:middle line:90%
So you don't earn that much.

00:20:47.860 --> 00:20:49.180 align:middle line:90%
You don't spend that much.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:53.980 align:middle line:84%
So you need to plan accordingly
of your personal portfolio.

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:57.060 align:middle line:84%
So that's related to the
question of time horizon.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:01.530 align:middle line:84%
And also, the marginal benefit
of having more wealth and money

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:07.780 align:middle line:84%
starts to diminish when you
have had certain minimum amount.

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.590 align:middle line:90%
So that's the bottom curve.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:14.440 align:middle line:90%
Your benefits start to plateau.

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:19.360 align:middle line:84%
And so also, do you want to take
more risk or not at that point?

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:23.750 align:middle line:84%
But for organization or
endowment on the right,

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:28.950 align:middle line:84%
it's a little different because
it's a perpetual portfolio.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:31.920 align:middle line:90%
It's a very long time horizon.

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:37.800 align:middle line:84%
And the spending keeps going
up as inflation goes up.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:43.410 align:middle line:84%
So you cannot basically
be complacent and say,

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:46.610 align:middle line:84%
I just put all the portfolio
into some conservative

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:47.700 align:middle line:90%
investments.

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:52.520 align:middle line:84%
You have to keep producing
those kind of returns.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:55.830 align:middle line:84%
So the endowment is
setup for university--

00:21:55.830 --> 00:22:01.580 align:middle line:84%
typically, they spend
5% of the portfolio.

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:06.570 align:middle line:84%
And on top of that, there's
an expected 3% inflation.

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:12.740 align:middle line:84%
So the nominal
return target is 8%.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:15.860 align:middle line:84%
So as you know,
it's probably not

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:19.550 align:middle line:84%
always easy to make
8% consistently year

00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:24.080 align:middle line:84%
over year, especially
when interest rate is low.

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:25.340 align:middle line:90%
You don't have a bond.

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:28.490 align:middle line:84%
There's safety bond
that you can put in.

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:34.460 align:middle line:84%
Also, endowment tends to have
a long time horizon, definitely

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:35.580 align:middle line:90%
more than 10 years.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:38.880 align:middle line:84%
That's what the
people always say.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:42.270 align:middle line:84%
Universities have hundreds
of years of history

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:48.230 align:middle line:84%
and looking to have another
many more hundreds of years.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:51.480 align:middle line:84%
And on the university's
spending budget,

00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:57.860 align:middle line:84%
operating budget, now, roughly
about 40% of the budget

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:01.050 align:middle line:84%
is coming from
endowment spending.

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:05.540 align:middle line:84%
So you can see universities
are now more and more relying

00:23:05.540 --> 00:23:07.830 align:middle line:90%
on endowment returns.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:15.320 align:middle line:84%
And that's because the research
grants from federal agencies

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:17.750 align:middle line:84%
and the industries
are coming down.

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:22.510 align:middle line:84%
So this has to be made up
from the investment portfolio.

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:27.160 align:middle line:84%
And so this is just a
list of the strategies

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:29.690 align:middle line:90%
endowments tend to invest in.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:33.370 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned cash, government
bonds, corporate bonds,

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:36.260 align:middle line:84%
and the credit products,
meaning the issuer,

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:40.280 align:middle line:84%
unlike the US government, it
has the risk of defaulting.

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:45.010 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, it will
pay you higher interest.

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:48.950 align:middle line:84%
So there are hedge funds
betting on the macro markets,

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:53.410 align:middle line:84%
betting on the Fed or stock
market, the general direction.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:57.980 align:middle line:84%
There are also quantitative
funds doing the trend following.

00:23:57.980 --> 00:24:02.110 align:middle line:84%
People usually call it CTA,
commodity trading advisors,

00:24:02.110 --> 00:24:09.430 align:middle line:84%
or stat arb, which is to
capture the relative value

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:13.700 align:middle line:84%
between a pair of stocks or
actually many pairs of stocks.

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:17.350 align:middle line:84%
So it's statistical
arbitrage, they call it,

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:23.190 align:middle line:84%
which is really to find
statistical patterns to generate

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:24.070 align:middle line:90%
returns.

00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:27.750 align:middle line:84%
So this is heavily
computer driven strategies.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:32.910 align:middle line:84%
And many big quantitative shops
are still doing a lot of these.

00:24:32.910 --> 00:24:35.920 align:middle line:84%
So there are fundamental
equity hedge funds.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:40.710 align:middle line:84%
They buy stocks and
also sell short stocks.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:44.430 align:middle line:84%
And they are also the
platforms you probably

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.890 align:middle line:84%
heard-- the big
platforms, they're

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:52.900 align:middle line:84%
called multi strat or multi PM,
portfolio manager, platforms.

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:58.800 align:middle line:84%
They hire hundreds of
different teams trading

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:03.090 align:middle line:84%
on the same platform in a
very different wide range

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:08.100 align:middle line:84%
of strategies, but achieve a
high degree of diversification.

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:13.280 align:middle line:84%
And they can level up the
portfolio quite a bit.

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:17.430 align:middle line:84%
And so I mentioned
private equity earlier.

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:20.760 align:middle line:84%
There are also real asset
investments-- real estate,

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:23.490 align:middle line:84%
natural resources,
farmland, timberland,

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:26.210 align:middle line:90%
those type of investments--

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:29.390 align:middle line:84%
and the new assets,
such as crypto,

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:33.870 align:middle line:84%
or intellectual properties,
or legal claims, and so on.

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:38.100 align:middle line:84%
So basically, anything you can
think of can generate a return.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:42.380 align:middle line:90%
It can also be considered.

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:45.260 align:middle line:84%
At the first lecture,
I already highlighted

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:48.810 align:middle line:84%
some of the features
of the endowment model.

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:51.590 align:middle line:84%
So endowment model
nowadays tends

00:25:51.590 --> 00:25:55.130 align:middle line:84%
to focus on hiring
external managers

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:59.120 align:middle line:84%
and doing both public
and private investing.

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:02.480 align:middle line:84%
And we hire both
generalists, who

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:06.410 align:middle line:84%
look at everything, and also
specialists in certain domains,

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:12.250 align:middle line:90%
such as biotech, AI, and so on.

00:26:12.250 --> 00:26:16.630 align:middle line:84%
We focus on both absolute
return and also relative

00:26:16.630 --> 00:26:19.060 align:middle line:90%
return to the benchmark.

00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:22.010 align:middle line:84%
So we are mostly
active managers.

00:26:22.010 --> 00:26:27.110 align:middle line:84%
So we have some passive
beta index investments.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:29.630 align:middle line:84%
But it's a minority
of the investment.

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:32.870 align:middle line:90%
It's also not always permanent.

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:36.910 align:middle line:84%
So whenever we can find an
active strategy or manager

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:40.540 align:middle line:84%
to beat a benchmark or
index, we will always

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:43.356 align:middle line:90%
go for the active investment.

00:26:43.356 --> 00:26:46.000 align:middle line:90%


00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:51.070 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so as you can see,
this investment process

00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:54.140 align:middle line:84%
is centered around
manager selection,

00:26:54.140 --> 00:27:00.140 align:middle line:84%
selecting the good
investing fund managers.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:03.250 align:middle line:84%
And Harvard, actually
in the old days,

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:06.950 align:middle line:84%
used to have a lot of
internal fund managers.

00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:10.240 align:middle line:84%
But that model has changed
from seven years ago

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:15.100 align:middle line:84%
to focus more on
external managers.

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:18.880 align:middle line:84%
So now, I'm going to
shift gears a little bit

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:22.970 align:middle line:84%
to talk about portfolio
construction problems,

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:25.240 align:middle line:84%
so shifting towards
the math part

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:27.760 align:middle line:90%
of the discussion a little bit.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:30.850 align:middle line:84%
So the classic portfolio
construction problem

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:32.570 align:middle line:90%
is stated as such--

00:27:32.570 --> 00:27:35.900 align:middle line:84%
assume you know your
return objectives.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.000 align:middle line:90%
You have a loss tolerance.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:43.250 align:middle line:84%
And you can predict each
investment's return volatility,

00:27:43.250 --> 00:27:47.770 align:middle line:84%
which is the standard deviation,
and also the correlation

00:27:47.770 --> 00:27:51.130 align:middle line:90%
matrix of these investments.

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:54.820 align:middle line:84%
The question is what
percentage you should

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:57.020 align:middle line:90%
put in each of the investments.

00:27:57.020 --> 00:28:00.820 align:middle line:84%
So that's the portfolio
construction problem.

00:28:00.820 --> 00:28:04.760 align:middle line:84%
And the objective of that,
as we mentioned earlier,

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:09.060 align:middle line:84%
is to maximize the return
for the whole portfolio,

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:12.010 align:middle line:84%
while minimizing
the portfolio risk.

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:16.260 align:middle line:84%
In this classic problem,
it's to minimize

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:21.960 align:middle line:84%
the uncertainty or volatility
of the whole portfolio.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:26.490 align:middle line:84%
So as you can see, the
portfolio construction problem

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:30.960 align:middle line:84%
really is a sizing
problem of investments

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:32.680 align:middle line:90%
at all different levels.

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:36.250 align:middle line:84%
You can relate that to
asset allocation problems.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:38.560 align:middle line:84%
So what is the asset
allocation problem?

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:43.120 align:middle line:84%
So you group those investments
into different asset classes.

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:48.070 align:middle line:84%
So as I showed you earlier,
the list of these strategies,

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:51.870 align:middle line:84%
you can also think of them
as lists of different asset

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:52.810 align:middle line:90%
classes.

00:28:52.810 --> 00:28:57.150 align:middle line:84%
So from top down, the
discussion needs to be had

00:28:57.150 --> 00:29:00.780 align:middle line:84%
is how much you want to be in
each category or each asset

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:02.880 align:middle line:90%
class.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:06.330 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, people
will say, well,

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:09.270 align:middle line:84%
that's still way too
many asset classes.

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:12.660 align:middle line:84%
If you have 10 to
15 asset classes,

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.990 align:middle line:84%
that makes the optimization
problem very hard.

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:22.130 align:middle line:84%
So people tend to reduce
them to big risk factors,

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:28.730 align:middle line:84%
sometimes, say, 3 to 5 risk
factors, usually equity, bonds,

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:35.460 align:middle line:84%
plus one of the other
things could be inflation.

00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:36.900 align:middle line:90%
It could be currency.

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:42.630 align:middle line:84%
It could be credit
and other dimensions.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:46.670 align:middle line:84%
But the factor analysis
can also expand

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:49.320 align:middle line:90%
into many detailed levels.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:54.750 align:middle line:84%
You can have 50 factors or
even hundreds of factors.

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:57.770 align:middle line:84%
But it's just different
ways of grouping

00:29:57.770 --> 00:30:01.310 align:middle line:84%
the investments to
make the optimization

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:03.754 align:middle line:90%
problem a little easier.

00:30:03.754 --> 00:30:06.940 align:middle line:84%
So then what is
the risk management

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:08.630 align:middle line:90%
problem of a portfolio?

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:12.820 align:middle line:84%
So risk management
problem, in many ways,

00:30:12.820 --> 00:30:15.860 align:middle line:84%
is actually the same problem
as portfolio construction.

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:17.540 align:middle line:90%
It's the sizing problem.

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:21.430 align:middle line:84%
But in addition, you
need to think about how

00:30:21.430 --> 00:30:23.750 align:middle line:90%
to avoid concentration.

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:27.550 align:middle line:84%
Concentration, of course, is
also a sizing problem and also

00:30:27.550 --> 00:30:28.900 align:middle line:90%
illiquidity.

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:34.270 align:middle line:84%
And what's unacceptable
losses and unwanted risks

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:36.916 align:middle line:90%
To constrain the portfolio?

00:30:36.916 --> 00:30:39.820 align:middle line:90%


00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:42.790 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
spend a few minutes

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:51.220 align:middle line:84%
on the portfolio theory, which
is illustrated in two assets.

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:56.170 align:middle line:84%
Asset 1 has a return,
R1; volatility, sigma 1;

00:30:56.170 --> 00:31:00.430 align:middle line:84%
and will have a weight
in the portfolio of w1,

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:02.440 align:middle line:90%
similarly for asset 2.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:04.740 align:middle line:84%
So the sum of the
two weights should

00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:08.130 align:middle line:90%
be equal to 1, w1 plus w2.

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:13.530 align:middle line:84%
And the portfolio return, which
is denoted here as R sub p,

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:18.720 align:middle line:84%
is the weighted
return of R1 and R2.

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:25.030 align:middle line:84%
And the variance-- or in this
here, it's the sigma p squared,

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:28.410 align:middle line:84%
the volatility squared,
can be expressed

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:34.140 align:middle line:84%
in terms of the weights and the
volatility of each of the two

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:38.130 align:middle line:84%
investments, as well as
the correlation, rho,

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:39.700 align:middle line:90%
between the two investments.

00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:42.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is a very
common equation

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:46.350 align:middle line:84%
you probably have
seen many times

00:31:46.350 --> 00:31:52.202 align:middle line:84%
in any probability or
statistics classes.

00:31:52.202 --> 00:31:54.780 align:middle line:90%


00:31:54.780 --> 00:32:00.540 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at
some special cases.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:05.280 align:middle line:84%
So when the correlation,
rho, is positive 1,

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:10.900 align:middle line:84%
meaning the two assets
are perfectly correlated,

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:15.670 align:middle line:84%
one moves up, the other moves up
at the same time, one goes down,

00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:17.740 align:middle line:84%
the other one goes
down at the same time.

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:22.050 align:middle line:84%
So that's called
perfect correlation.

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:31.290 align:middle line:84%
So if rho equals to 1 and we
can simplify the sigma p, which

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:34.710 align:middle line:84%
is the portfolio volatility,
it becomes the weighted sum

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:39.630 align:middle line:84%
of the two volatilities-- w1
times sigma 1 plus w2 times

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:40.800 align:middle line:90%
sigma 2.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:47.590 align:middle line:84%
Or you can also express the
w1 in terms of 1 minus w2.

00:32:47.590 --> 00:32:53.100 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, solving for
w2 in terms of sigma p

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:56.160 align:middle line:90%
and the sigma 1 and sigma 2.

00:32:56.160 --> 00:33:03.320 align:middle line:84%
So then you can derive the
overall return of the portfolio,

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:11.340 align:middle line:84%
R sub p, in terms of w2,
because w2 above is a function,

00:33:11.340 --> 00:33:14.690 align:middle line:90%
is a linear function of sigma p.

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:19.970 align:middle line:84%
So you know, in this case, the
return of the portfolio, Rp,

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:26.300 align:middle line:90%
is a linear function of sigma p.

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:31.010 align:middle line:84%
So on this chart, you can
see that straight line

00:33:31.010 --> 00:33:32.670 align:middle line:90%
when rho equals to 1.

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:34.700 align:middle line:90%
That's the special case.

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:41.330 align:middle line:84%
When rho equals to minus 1,
that means they are perfectly

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:43.260 align:middle line:90%
negatively correlated.

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:46.520 align:middle line:84%
So one goes up, the
other one goes down.

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:51.920 align:middle line:84%
So when that is the case, you
will actually easily derive

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:55.610 align:middle line:84%
that this sigma p
is actually-- you

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:58.190 align:middle line:90%
have two branches of solutions.

00:33:58.190 --> 00:34:04.930 align:middle line:84%
So it depends on the value of w1
sigma 1 compared to w2 sigma 2.

00:34:04.930 --> 00:34:11.800 align:middle line:84%
So that's the two separate
lines on the left.

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:13.550 align:middle line:90%
That's the other solution.

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:16.540 align:middle line:84%
In the middle is
the other values

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:20.830 align:middle line:84%
of the correlation between
negative 1 and plus 1,

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:25.740 align:middle line:84%
including 0 correlation,
rho equal to 0.

00:34:25.740 --> 00:34:29.199 align:middle line:90%


00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:35.949 align:middle line:84%
So what if the first asset has 0
volatility, sigma 1 equals to 0?

00:34:35.949 --> 00:34:40.940 align:middle line:84%
So then from the equation
earlier, we can derive sigma p,

00:34:40.940 --> 00:34:45.460 align:middle line:84%
the portfolio volatility
is simply w2 times sigma 2

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:47.650 align:middle line:90%
because sigma 1 is 0.

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:52.239 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, w2, the
weighting on the asset 2,

00:34:52.239 --> 00:34:57.060 align:middle line:90%
is sigma p divided by sigma 2.

00:34:57.060 --> 00:35:01.080 align:middle line:90%
That's a straightforward result.

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:05.760 align:middle line:84%
And the portfolio
return, R sub p,

00:35:05.760 --> 00:35:11.250 align:middle line:84%
now can be written
as R1, which is

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:14.880 align:middle line:84%
the first asset with 0
volatility, which you

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:16.960 align:middle line:90%
can think of as a cash right.

00:35:16.960 --> 00:35:19.480 align:middle line:84%
We mentioned earlier,
cash has zero volatility.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:23.590 align:middle line:84%
So this cash or riskless
asset has return R1.

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:26.400 align:middle line:90%


00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:33.810 align:middle line:84%
Its Rp is still a linear
function of sigma p.

00:35:33.810 --> 00:35:37.360 align:middle line:84%
But it's a straight
line, in this case.

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:41.130 align:middle line:84%
You can see the
slope of that line

00:35:41.130 --> 00:35:45.930 align:middle line:84%
is determined by the
difference of R2 minus R1

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:50.280 align:middle line:90%
and divided by sigma 2.

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:56.180 align:middle line:84%
So by the way, this is so-called
capital allocation line

00:35:56.180 --> 00:35:59.700 align:middle line:90%
in the portfolio theory.

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:04.890 align:middle line:84%
So we can look at, let's
say, a constructed portfolio.

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:06.770 align:middle line:84%
You already have a
sigma p, which has

00:36:06.770 --> 00:36:10.310 align:middle line:90%
many assets in it, and also Rp.

00:36:10.310 --> 00:36:19.040 align:middle line:84%
By adding one riskless asset,
or cash, which returns Rf.

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:23.880 align:middle line:84%
Rf is basically like
a risk free return.

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:30.120 align:middle line:84%
You can draw a tangent line
over the sigma P point.

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:35.280 align:middle line:84%
You pick the one point on
that efficient frontier.

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:37.260 align:middle line:84%
I should explain
efficient frontier,

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:41.210 align:middle line:84%
meaning on that return
versus volatility chart,

00:36:41.210 --> 00:36:44.130 align:middle line:84%
because you have
different weightings--

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:46.790 align:middle line:90%
so let's come back to this.

00:36:46.790 --> 00:36:51.590 align:middle line:90%
Let's look at this curve here.

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:54.680 align:middle line:84%
So for different
w1 and w2's, you

00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:57.380 align:middle line:84%
can have different
resulting portfolio return

00:36:57.380 --> 00:36:59.070 align:middle line:90%
and the portfolio volatility.

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:03.230 align:middle line:84%
So this line, the
upper part of this

00:37:03.230 --> 00:37:08.420 align:middle line:84%
is called the efficient frontier
because by lowering your risk

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:12.780 align:middle line:84%
and moving your return
up, that's your objective.

00:37:12.780 --> 00:37:14.670 align:middle line:90%
You are improving the portfolio.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:19.460 align:middle line:84%
So any point on this line
is a potential solution

00:37:19.460 --> 00:37:22.550 align:middle line:90%
of that portfolio construction.

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:31.850 align:middle line:84%
So when we add the risk
free asset on this Rf point,

00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:35.900 align:middle line:84%
we can basically combine
that risk free asset

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:42.680 align:middle line:84%
with the existing portfolio
to further improve the risk--

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:47.810 align:middle line:84%
the efficient frontier to
move up to that straight line.

00:37:47.810 --> 00:37:52.540 align:middle line:84%
So that slope, Rp minus
RF divided by sigma p

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:54.550 align:middle line:90%
is so-called Sharpe ratio.

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:59.000 align:middle line:84%
In simple terms, think about
your return over volatility.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:05.080 align:middle line:84%
So that's the Sharpe
ratio definition.

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:09.370 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, you also hear
the term alpha and beta.

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:12.020 align:middle line:84%
That's compared
with a benchmark.

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:15.790 align:middle line:84%
So in this case,
you can write down

00:38:15.790 --> 00:38:19.270 align:middle line:84%
the portfolio return minus
the risk-free asset in terms

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:22.600 align:middle line:84%
of alpha plus beta
times benchmark

00:38:22.600 --> 00:38:25.030 align:middle line:90%
minus the risk-free return.

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:30.280 align:middle line:84%
And the beta is a
function of correlation

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:32.080 align:middle line:84%
of the portfolio
to the benchmark

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:34.610 align:middle line:90%
and the ratio of the volatility.

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:38.470 align:middle line:90%


00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:46.510 align:middle line:84%
What if you want to level up
the portfolio and borrow more,

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:50.490 align:middle line:84%
meaning make the weight of
the first risk-free asset

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:56.920 align:middle line:84%
to be minus 100% and make
the other asset be 200%,

00:38:56.920 --> 00:38:59.020 align:middle line:90%
so still sum up to 1?

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:04.710 align:middle line:84%
So that means you're
borrowing money in cash.

00:39:04.710 --> 00:39:09.040 align:middle line:84%
You're putting the proceeds
into the risky asset.

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:16.080 align:middle line:84%
So you're moving the
volatility up to this point

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:19.260 align:middle line:84%
by investing more
into the second asset.

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:21.550 align:middle line:90%
So that's also possible.

00:39:21.550 --> 00:39:27.430 align:middle line:84%
That's a typical exercise people
do in risk parity portfolios.

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:31.660 align:middle line:84%
By the way, if you don't follow
any of those, that's fine.

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:33.480 align:middle line:84%
The reason I want
to talk about this

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:37.620 align:middle line:84%
is really to give you some
flavors of what the portfolio

00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:44.130 align:middle line:84%
theory is about because then I'm
going to tell you, in practice,

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:48.200 align:middle line:84%
a lot of these need
to be improved.

00:39:48.200 --> 00:39:52.040 align:middle line:84%
So the example of portfolio with
two assets that we talked about

00:39:52.040 --> 00:39:54.720 align:middle line:90%
is the 60/40 equity bonds.

00:39:54.720 --> 00:40:00.500 align:middle line:84%
Some of you wrote down
70 bonds, 30% stocks.

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:05.420 align:middle line:84%
That's very much similar to the
risk parity portfolio, which

00:40:05.420 --> 00:40:09.020 align:middle line:84%
they try to make equal
risk contribution coming

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:13.790 align:middle line:84%
from the bonds and
equity by borrowing more

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:18.110 align:middle line:84%
to invest in the bonds because
bonds typically have lower

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:19.710 align:middle line:90%
volatility than equity.

00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:24.350 align:middle line:84%
So you need to have more to
make them have equal risk

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:25.230 align:middle line:90%
contribution.

00:40:25.230 --> 00:40:26.400 align:middle line:90%
So that's the idea.

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:30.320 align:middle line:84%
Again, today, I'm not
going into the math.

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:33.390 align:middle line:84%
In the previous years, sometimes
I spent more time on that.

00:40:33.390 --> 00:40:40.010 align:middle line:84%
But I feel I want to talk about
other things a bit more today.

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:44.050 align:middle line:84%
But before we move away, I want
to talk about the importance

00:40:44.050 --> 00:40:47.560 align:middle line:90%
of rebalancing.

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:53.900 align:middle line:84%
Because we had this 2013 OCW
video, just two weeks ago,

00:40:53.900 --> 00:40:58.810 align:middle line:84%
I think somebody made a
clip out of that class,

00:40:58.810 --> 00:41:06.310 align:middle line:84%
sped it up to 1.5 times,
and made it a 3-minute video

00:41:06.310 --> 00:41:10.760 align:middle line:84%
of the only free lunch
of diversification,

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:13.400 align:middle line:90%
which is exactly this example.

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:16.490 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to go through
it with you quickly.

00:41:16.490 --> 00:41:22.540 align:middle line:84%
Just think about
you have two assets,

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:24.460 align:middle line:90%
assets in a two-year period.

00:41:24.460 --> 00:41:27.550 align:middle line:84%
In year 1, the
first one doubles.

00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:30.380 align:middle line:90%
And then in year 2, it halves.

00:41:30.380 --> 00:41:35.240 align:middle line:84%
So I think it's a little easier
to show on the blackboard.

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:39.460 align:middle line:84%
But the other one
does the opposite.

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:46.930 align:middle line:90%


00:41:46.930 --> 00:41:49.130 align:middle line:90%
So they both started here.

00:41:49.130 --> 00:41:53.290 align:middle line:84%
The first asset
doubles, then halves.

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:56.170 align:middle line:90%
So this is year 1.

00:41:56.170 --> 00:41:58.120 align:middle line:90%
This is year 2.

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:03.460 align:middle line:84%
The second asset halves
first, then doubles.

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:07.180 align:middle line:90%
So those were the R1 and R2's.

00:42:07.180 --> 00:42:13.900 align:middle line:84%
So if you start as equal weight
of the two, and after year 1,

00:42:13.900 --> 00:42:15.310 align:middle line:90%
you can calculate.

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.880 align:middle line:90%
The portfolio is up 25%.

00:42:18.880 --> 00:42:21.020 align:middle line:90%
So this is just simple math.

00:42:21.020 --> 00:42:25.520 align:middle line:90%
The first asset 1 is 50% weight.

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:27.550 align:middle line:90%
Asset 2 is also 50% weight.

00:42:27.550 --> 00:42:29.200 align:middle line:90%
One doubles, one halves.

00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:35.470 align:middle line:84%
So total, you made 25% But
if you don't do anything

00:42:35.470 --> 00:42:40.710 align:middle line:84%
at this point, asset 1 will
be dominating the portfolio.

00:42:40.710 --> 00:42:44.520 align:middle line:84%
Asset 2 will be just
a small portion.

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.320 align:middle line:84%
Then because asset
2 then doubles,

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:49.390 align:middle line:84%
you're coming back
to the same point.

00:42:49.390 --> 00:42:52.170 align:middle line:84%
After two years, your
portfolio's return

00:42:52.170 --> 00:42:56.340 align:middle line:84%
is exactly 0%,
even though you had

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:59.970 align:middle line:84%
a diversified portfolio
with perfectly

00:42:59.970 --> 00:43:03.360 align:middle line:90%
negatively correlated assets.

00:43:03.360 --> 00:43:09.780 align:middle line:84%
But if you rebalance at
this point to equal weight,

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:12.690 align:middle line:84%
then your portfolio
in year 2 will make

00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:16.800 align:middle line:90%
another 25% return in year 2.

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:19.800 align:middle line:84%
So you're making
compounded return

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:23.880 align:middle line:90%
of 25% two years in a row.

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:28.410 align:middle line:84%
So some of you may point out
that isn't this just betting

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:32.040 align:middle line:90%
on mean reversion?

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:37.050 align:middle line:84%
And if you think it's a trend
trade, you shouldn't do this.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.410 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

00:43:39.410 --> 00:43:41.550 align:middle line:84%
The question you
should ask yourself,

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:47.970 align:middle line:84%
what is the basis of your sizing
or weighting of the two assets?

00:43:47.970 --> 00:43:51.420 align:middle line:84%
So you started with
an equal weighting,

00:43:51.420 --> 00:43:54.410 align:middle line:84%
meaning that you think
the two assets have

00:43:54.410 --> 00:43:57.890 align:middle line:84%
the same likelihood
of performing

00:43:57.890 --> 00:43:59.430 align:middle line:90%
and the same amount of risk.

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:02.600 align:middle line:84%
That's why you
size them equally.

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:07.370 align:middle line:84%
And of course, you also know
they are negatively correlated.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:12.350 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of year 1, do
you maintain that view or not?

00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:18.090 align:middle line:84%
If you think it's still the same
projection of risk and return,

00:44:18.090 --> 00:44:20.880 align:middle line:84%
then you should maintain
the equal weight.

00:44:20.880 --> 00:44:25.880 align:middle line:84%
You should not change the
weighting unless, at the year 1,

00:44:25.880 --> 00:44:28.320 align:middle line:84%
your projection has
totally changed.

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:34.130 align:middle line:84%
You think asset 1 will
continue to outperform asset 2.

00:44:34.130 --> 00:44:38.580 align:middle line:84%
Yes, in that case, you can
change to a non-equal weighting.

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:39.960 align:middle line:90%
So that's the point.

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:44.780 align:middle line:84%
If you believe they have
the same probability

00:44:44.780 --> 00:44:49.980 align:middle line:84%
distribution, then there's no
reason for you not to rebalance.

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:51.840 align:middle line:84%
So people say
there's a free lunch.

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:54.180 align:middle line:84%
The only free lunch
is diversification.

00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:57.560 align:middle line:84%
But you have to
rebalance to eat it.

00:44:57.560 --> 00:45:00.440 align:middle line:90%
So that's on that.

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:02.840 align:middle line:84%
So then I'm going
to shift the gear

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:07.910 align:middle line:84%
to talk about the limitations
of so-called modern portfolio

00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:10.760 align:middle line:90%
theory, in short MPT.

00:45:10.760 --> 00:45:14.870 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned this was mostly
developed in the 1950s.

00:45:14.870 --> 00:45:19.740 align:middle line:84%
And Harry Markowitz
pioneered in this area.

00:45:19.740 --> 00:45:22.620 align:middle line:84%
He actually got a Nobel
Prize in the, I think,

00:45:22.620 --> 00:45:26.420 align:middle line:90%
early '90s for his work.

00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:31.260 align:middle line:84%
So all I have just
described to you so far,

00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:36.194 align:middle line:84%
the mathematical part, has
a lot to do with his work.

00:45:36.194 --> 00:45:40.480 align:middle line:84%
But you can tell
already, a lot of

00:45:40.480 --> 00:45:44.860 align:middle line:84%
these depend on your confidence
in the capital market

00:45:44.860 --> 00:45:50.440 align:middle line:84%
assumptions, which is the
projections of return volatility

00:45:50.440 --> 00:45:54.871 align:middle line:84%
and the correlation matrix
of these investments.

00:45:54.871 --> 00:45:58.690 align:middle line:84%
And the mean variance
optimization problem

00:45:58.690 --> 00:46:02.030 align:middle line:84%
often has unlimited
number of solutions.

00:46:02.030 --> 00:46:05.470 align:middle line:84%
And you have to put
artificial constraints

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:07.460 align:middle line:90%
to bound the solutions.

00:46:07.460 --> 00:46:12.850 align:middle line:84%
They are very sensitive to small
change of these capital market

00:46:12.850 --> 00:46:15.280 align:middle line:90%
assumptions.

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:18.440 align:middle line:84%
So in practice, it's
very hard to use it.

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:21.880 align:middle line:90%
Probably not many people use it.

00:46:21.880 --> 00:46:27.010 align:middle line:84%
So with those
questions, I myself

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:29.050 align:middle line:84%
have always been
curious about this

00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:33.790 align:middle line:84%
and have done various
research work in this.

00:46:33.790 --> 00:46:37.440 align:middle line:84%
So today, I'm going
to highlight a few.

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:41.400 align:middle line:84%
The first one is more related
to the sizing question,

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.753 align:middle line:90%
using a different approach.

00:46:43.753 --> 00:46:46.410 align:middle line:90%


00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:50.490 align:middle line:90%
I call it a gain-loss ratio.

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:52.710 align:middle line:84%
The second part,
I will talk a bit

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:58.030 align:middle line:84%
about the effect of crowding
behavior and how to model it.

00:46:58.030 --> 00:47:05.460 align:middle line:84%
And lastly, I will talk about
the power law distribution

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:08.500 align:middle line:84%
coming out of these
crowd interactions,

00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:14.220 align:middle line:84%
why these are all relevant
to portfolio construction.

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:19.170 align:middle line:84%
So issue number 1 for
the MPT, volatility

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:21.210 align:middle line:90%
is a poor measure of risk.

00:47:21.210 --> 00:47:22.750 align:middle line:90%
Why do I say that?

00:47:22.750 --> 00:47:28.320 align:middle line:84%
Because volatility is just
the standard deviation

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:31.220 align:middle line:90%
or the range of uncertainties.

00:47:31.220 --> 00:47:36.480 align:middle line:84%
So if I am long, out of
the money call option--

00:47:36.480 --> 00:47:37.230 align:middle line:90%
think about it.

00:47:37.230 --> 00:47:39.030 align:middle line:90%
You have one investment.

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:42.930 align:middle line:84%
And Steven, you
worked on options.

00:47:42.930 --> 00:47:46.550 align:middle line:84%
You probably can
get this easily.

00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:48.840 align:middle line:84%
If I'm not out of
the money option,

00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:52.615 align:middle line:84%
do I wish the volatility
to go up or down?

00:47:52.615 --> 00:47:56.240 align:middle line:90%


00:47:56.240 --> 00:47:59.190 align:middle line:84%
Do I want my portfolio to
have high vol or low vol?

00:47:59.190 --> 00:48:02.050 align:middle line:90%
I want my portfolio-- yes, yeah?

00:48:02.050 --> 00:48:03.050 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: The higher vol.

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:04.290 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yes, right.

00:48:04.290 --> 00:48:05.610 align:middle line:90%
The higher vol, the better.

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So on the other hand, if I'm
short on the money option,

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:14.780 align:middle line:84%
I probably want to have
lower vol because I

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:17.270 align:middle line:90%
don't want to get exercised.

00:48:17.270 --> 00:48:23.720 align:middle line:84%
So it really depends on your
gain situation, your payout,

00:48:23.720 --> 00:48:26.450 align:middle line:90%
or your potential loss.

00:48:26.450 --> 00:48:30.830 align:middle line:84%
And the Sharpe ratio is
derived from volatility.

00:48:30.830 --> 00:48:34.240 align:middle line:84%
So basically, it suffers
from the same issue.

00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:36.445 align:middle line:84%
So over the years,
there are methods,

00:48:36.445 --> 00:48:41.830 align:middle line:84%
like the Sortino ratio,
that try to differentiate

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:45.970 align:middle line:84%
the upside range and
the downside range.

00:48:45.970 --> 00:48:48.490 align:middle line:90%
I think it's a good measurement.

00:48:48.490 --> 00:48:55.010 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't give you
a direct link to sizing.

00:48:55.010 --> 00:48:57.690 align:middle line:84%
The ultimate question we
need to answer is sizing.

00:48:57.690 --> 00:49:00.220 align:middle line:90%


00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:04.870 align:middle line:84%
So the new way to compare
different investments

00:49:04.870 --> 00:49:09.970 align:middle line:84%
I'm basically using,
or I came up with,

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:13.910 align:middle line:84%
is to use expected
gain and expected loss.

00:49:13.910 --> 00:49:18.470 align:middle line:84%
I call the G and L. Here are
both use positive numbers.

00:49:18.470 --> 00:49:22.150 align:middle line:84%
So when I say loss, these
are absolute numbers.

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:27.360 align:middle line:84%
So we are looking for
the best skill, which

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:33.330 align:middle line:84%
is getting much bigger
expected gain, much bigger

00:49:33.330 --> 00:49:36.370 align:middle line:90%
than the expected loss.

00:49:36.370 --> 00:49:40.530 align:middle line:84%
Or written in different
forms, in the G minus

00:49:40.530 --> 00:49:45.720 align:middle line:84%
L, which is the return,
divided by GA plus L.

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:48.370 align:middle line:84%
And you change the
format a little bit.

00:49:48.370 --> 00:49:52.530 align:middle line:84%
It becomes 1 minus 2L
divided by G plus L.

00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:59.070 align:middle line:84%
This is really exactly
the sizing percentage from

00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:04.440 align:middle line:84%
Kelly's criteria in
the binary betting.

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:09.370 align:middle line:84%
So this number is bounded
from minus 1 to plus 1.

00:50:09.370 --> 00:50:15.060 align:middle line:84%
So it can be directly
translated into sizing.

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:19.320 align:middle line:84%
And so let me give
you a few examples

00:50:19.320 --> 00:50:21.940 align:middle line:90%
to help you understand this.

00:50:21.940 --> 00:50:26.400 align:middle line:84%
So if you are
flipping a coin, you

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:30.630 align:middle line:84%
know the head has a
certain probability

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:32.920 align:middle line:84%
and the tail has a
certain probability.

00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:37.600 align:middle line:84%
And when it's a head, it has
a payout you will get or lose.

00:50:37.600 --> 00:50:40.000 align:middle line:84%
And when it's tail, you
have a payout or lose.

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:42.570 align:middle line:84%
So expected gain
is the probability

00:50:42.570 --> 00:50:45.670 align:middle line:84%
of the head times the
payout, if you bet on head.

00:50:45.670 --> 00:50:49.560 align:middle line:84%
Expected loss is the
probability of the tail

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:52.540 align:middle line:90%
times the payout on that side.

00:50:52.540 --> 00:50:56.710 align:middle line:84%
So in that discrete example,
it's fairly easy to understand.

00:50:56.710 --> 00:51:01.950 align:middle line:84%
But in the continuous example,
it's the terminal distribution.

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:07.800 align:middle line:84%
Let's say you have a probability
density function describing

00:51:07.800 --> 00:51:09.750 align:middle line:90%
all the outcomes.

00:51:09.750 --> 00:51:12.570 align:middle line:84%
Then you need to use
that probability density

00:51:12.570 --> 00:51:18.600 align:middle line:84%
function times the payout
integrated over all the losing

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:21.330 align:middle line:84%
parts and integrated
over all the

00:51:21.330 --> 00:51:25.010 align:middle line:84%
gaining parts to get the
expected loss and the expected

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:25.770 align:middle line:90%
gain.

00:51:25.770 --> 00:51:32.490 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't have to be
always separated by 0.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:37.680 align:middle line:84%
If you have a target return,
you integrate over, say,

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:41.580 align:middle line:84%
from minus infinity to
the targeted return,

00:51:41.580 --> 00:51:43.980 align:middle line:90%
that's your expected loss.

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:47.900 align:middle line:84%
That is what needs
to be included in L.

00:51:47.900 --> 00:51:52.430 align:middle line:84%
So when you do this,
your optimization problem

00:51:52.430 --> 00:51:56.910 align:middle line:84%
is no longer return
versus volatility.

00:51:56.910 --> 00:52:00.200 align:middle line:84%
But in the vertical
axis is gain.

00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:03.870 align:middle line:84%
In the horizontal
axis, L is loss.

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:09.020 align:middle line:84%
So you need to
basically maximize G

00:52:09.020 --> 00:52:16.370 align:middle line:84%
while minimizing L. So this is
all relevant because, as I just

00:52:16.370 --> 00:52:20.840 align:middle line:84%
explained earlier, minimizing
volatility is not always

00:52:20.840 --> 00:52:22.210 align:middle line:90%
for your benefit.

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:25.960 align:middle line:84%
When you are long option, you
need to have higher volatility.

00:52:25.960 --> 00:52:30.200 align:middle line:84%
But in this case, when
you specify expected loss,

00:52:30.200 --> 00:52:36.400 align:middle line:84%
that's what you need to budget
and control your investment.

00:52:36.400 --> 00:52:41.530 align:middle line:84%
So in the investment game we
gave you at the beginning,

00:52:41.530 --> 00:52:46.630 align:middle line:84%
I hope you all have submitted
your choices in September.

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:51.880 align:middle line:84%
And some of you
rebalanced in October.

00:52:51.880 --> 00:52:57.010 align:middle line:84%
I wanted you to track the
daily gain and the loss

00:52:57.010 --> 00:52:59.320 align:middle line:90%
in dollar amount.

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:04.670 align:middle line:84%
And at the end of the game,
which is mid-November,

00:53:04.670 --> 00:53:10.210 align:middle line:84%
I want you to summarize
all the daily gains

00:53:10.210 --> 00:53:15.890 align:middle line:84%
and also summarize the daily
losses to derive the G and L.

00:53:15.890 --> 00:53:19.250 align:middle line:84%
So some of you said, in
order to win this game,

00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:21.360 align:middle line:84%
why don't I just pick
the highest volatility

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:29.380 align:middle line:84%
stock to have the best potential
to produce highest return?

00:53:29.380 --> 00:53:30.840 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:53:30.840 --> 00:53:34.440 align:middle line:84%
But your higher
volatility investment

00:53:34.440 --> 00:53:40.290 align:middle line:84%
will suffer on the denominator,
which the G plus L--

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:45.180 align:middle line:84%
this ratio will indicate the
quality of your investment,

00:53:45.180 --> 00:53:47.820 align:middle line:84%
even though your G minus
L, which is the return,

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:48.910 align:middle line:90%
may be good.

00:53:48.910 --> 00:53:51.210 align:middle line:84%
But the denominator
will normalize

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:57.360 align:middle line:84%
it to make it basically
comparable to other investments.

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:59.740 align:middle line:90%
So the optimization problem--

00:53:59.740 --> 00:54:01.200 align:middle line:90%
yes, please.

00:54:01.200 --> 00:54:03.510 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So in
practice, what do you

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:05.560 align:middle line:84%
use to estimate
the expected gain?

00:54:05.560 --> 00:54:06.810 align:middle line:90%
[SNEEZE]

00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:08.010 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yeah, bless you.

00:54:08.010 --> 00:54:12.480 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE] estimate
the distribution of the pricing?

00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:13.590 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yes.

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:18.930 align:middle line:84%
But no more difficult than
getting the return and standard

00:54:18.930 --> 00:54:20.760 align:middle line:90%
deviation.

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:23.310 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so it's the
same amount of work.

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:25.800 align:middle line:84%
It's just that you're
separating the numbers.

00:54:25.800 --> 00:54:28.170 align:middle line:84%
You're calculating half
distribution instead

00:54:28.170 --> 00:54:30.810 align:middle line:90%
of the whole distribution.

00:54:30.810 --> 00:54:31.920 align:middle line:90%
So it is.

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:36.450 align:middle line:84%
So especially when you do the
optimization problem, as I'm

00:54:36.450 --> 00:54:40.200 align:middle line:90%
about to explain, these lines--

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:45.060 align:middle line:84%
basically, for example, the
45-degree line indicates G minus

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:47.950 align:middle line:90%
L equal to 0, so 0 return line.

00:54:47.950 --> 00:54:54.240 align:middle line:84%
So you don't want to pick any
investments below that 45-degree

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:55.000 align:middle line:90%
line.

00:54:55.000 --> 00:54:59.070 align:middle line:84%
You want the lines that have
higher slope, indicating

00:54:59.070 --> 00:55:01.620 align:middle line:90%
higher G over L ratio.

00:55:01.620 --> 00:55:04.590 align:middle line:84%
But when you combine
them, combine,

00:55:04.590 --> 00:55:09.480 align:middle line:84%
let's say, two assets
into one, calculating

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:13.320 align:middle line:84%
the combined portfolio G
and L, which is actually

00:55:13.320 --> 00:55:17.420 align:middle line:84%
path dependent, which
actually should be because,

00:55:17.420 --> 00:55:22.070 align:middle line:84%
when you have two
investments, the final outcome

00:55:22.070 --> 00:55:24.740 align:middle line:84%
of the expected
gain and the loss

00:55:24.740 --> 00:55:27.553 align:middle line:90%
indeed will be path dependent.

00:55:27.553 --> 00:55:28.970 align:middle line:84%
That means you
probably need to do

00:55:28.970 --> 00:55:33.390 align:middle line:84%
a lot of Monte Carlo
simulation to derive that.

00:55:33.390 --> 00:55:35.840 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, but it's no
more work amount

00:55:35.840 --> 00:55:40.946 align:middle line:84%
than the traditional
distribution calculation.

00:55:40.946 --> 00:55:42.537 align:middle line:90%


00:55:42.537 --> 00:55:44.720 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:55:44.720 --> 00:55:52.400 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's move on to modern
portfolio theory issue number 2.

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:55.650 align:middle line:84%
How do we predict the
future based on the past?

00:55:55.650 --> 00:56:01.280 align:middle line:84%
How confident can we be in the
capital market assumptions--

00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:04.970 align:middle line:84%
return, volatility,
and the correlation?

00:56:04.970 --> 00:56:08.960 align:middle line:84%
So because we human
thinking process--

00:56:08.960 --> 00:56:12.350 align:middle line:84%
our thinking process is
always extracting patterns

00:56:12.350 --> 00:56:13.800 align:middle line:90%
from the data.

00:56:13.800 --> 00:56:15.520 align:middle line:90%
So we have observations.

00:56:15.520 --> 00:56:18.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm just outlining the
typical thinking process.

00:56:18.380 --> 00:56:20.620 align:middle line:90%
You have observation.

00:56:20.620 --> 00:56:22.790 align:middle line:90%
You have measurable data.

00:56:22.790 --> 00:56:24.260 align:middle line:90%
Try to quantify them.

00:56:24.260 --> 00:56:27.730 align:middle line:84%
Then you try to extract
useful information

00:56:27.730 --> 00:56:30.760 align:middle line:90%
or recognize patterns.

00:56:30.760 --> 00:56:34.870 align:middle line:84%
Then you try to build the
models, having inputs, outputs

00:56:34.870 --> 00:56:36.280 align:middle line:90%
and conditions.

00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:41.560 align:middle line:84%
Then you predict output from
the inputs and the conditions.

00:56:41.560 --> 00:56:44.620 align:middle line:84%
Then you repeat
your observation.

00:56:44.620 --> 00:56:49.270 align:middle line:84%
Then you iterate, try to
calibrate your model parameters.

00:56:49.270 --> 00:56:50.830 align:middle line:84%
When you are very
good at it, you

00:56:50.830 --> 00:56:53.110 align:middle line:84%
can actually control
conditions and inputs

00:56:53.110 --> 00:56:56.270 align:middle line:90%
to get the desired outcome.

00:56:56.270 --> 00:57:01.490 align:middle line:90%
So that's true for any subject--

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:05.810 align:middle line:90%
physics, engineering, finance.

00:57:05.810 --> 00:57:09.590 align:middle line:84%
But finance involves
human behaviors,

00:57:09.590 --> 00:57:12.540 align:middle line:90%
which is much more complicated.

00:57:12.540 --> 00:57:17.170 align:middle line:84%
So the historical patterns
may not be repeatable.

00:57:17.170 --> 00:57:21.660 align:middle line:84%
And also, the crowding behavior
and also adaptive behavior

00:57:21.660 --> 00:57:26.010 align:middle line:84%
of human players will make the
process much more complicated.

00:57:26.010 --> 00:57:27.783 align:middle line:90%
So I want to show you a video.

00:57:27.783 --> 00:57:30.600 align:middle line:90%


00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:32.105 align:middle line:90%
Let's see if this works.

00:57:32.105 --> 00:58:03.000 align:middle line:90%


00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:08.790 align:middle line:84%
So you are watching is
a video of birds or bats

00:58:08.790 --> 00:58:18.950 align:middle line:84%
in a totally is self-organized
way to form a group behavior.

00:58:18.950 --> 00:58:23.090 align:middle line:84%
What they do is they
watch their neighbors.

00:58:23.090 --> 00:58:27.020 align:middle line:90%
They adjust their actions.

00:58:27.020 --> 00:58:33.593 align:middle line:84%
And the group as a whole
displays certain patterns.

00:58:33.593 --> 00:58:38.390 align:middle line:90%


00:58:38.390 --> 00:58:46.610 align:middle line:84%
The financial market is very
similar to this, except there's

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:50.450 align:middle line:84%
a central observation
you can actually see,

00:58:50.450 --> 00:58:52.640 align:middle line:90%
which is the stock price.

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:56.640 align:middle line:84%
You can also look at
your neighbors, too.

00:58:56.640 --> 00:58:58.610 align:middle line:84%
But nowadays, it's
much easier to look

00:58:58.610 --> 00:59:00.376 align:middle line:90%
at the central observation.

00:59:00.376 --> 00:59:05.690 align:middle line:90%


00:59:05.690 --> 00:59:12.260 align:middle line:84%
In the past, I've also shown
the London Bridge video.

00:59:12.260 --> 00:59:16.420 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have heard the
Millennium Bridge of London?

00:59:16.420 --> 00:59:17.230 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:17.960 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:21.830 align:middle line:84%
It opened, the Millennium
Bridge, around 2000.

00:59:21.830 --> 00:59:27.590 align:middle line:84%
On the first day, when people
walked on it, it starts to sway.

00:59:27.590 --> 00:59:31.430 align:middle line:84%
And then people tried
to keep their balance.

00:59:31.430 --> 00:59:33.430 align:middle line:84%
They become
synchronized in steps.

00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:36.400 align:middle line:90%
That made the sway even bigger.

00:59:36.400 --> 00:59:39.830 align:middle line:90%
So market does the same thing.

00:59:39.830 --> 00:59:45.110 align:middle line:84%
When panic feeds into panic
or greed feeds into greed,

00:59:45.110 --> 00:59:48.460 align:middle line:84%
market can form bubbles
and the crashing.

00:59:48.460 --> 00:59:55.640 align:middle line:84%
And these kind of behaviors
are not easily predictable.

00:59:55.640 --> 00:59:59.500 align:middle line:84%
But it's part of
crowding behavior.

00:59:59.500 --> 01:00:05.200 align:middle line:84%
So how to understand and
model this kind of behavior?

01:00:05.200 --> 01:00:08.450 align:middle line:84%
So we can look at it
as a feedback loop.

01:00:08.450 --> 01:00:14.410 align:middle line:90%
So S here is the agent's action.

01:00:14.410 --> 01:00:16.810 align:middle line:90%
O is the observation.

01:00:16.810 --> 01:00:22.810 align:middle line:84%
A is kind of an amplifier or
agent's action fed through

01:00:22.810 --> 01:00:25.310 align:middle line:90%
into the observation.

01:00:25.310 --> 01:00:28.150 align:middle line:84%
As you can see, the
change of the observation

01:00:28.150 --> 01:00:34.610 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of the agent's action
times the power they have,

01:00:34.610 --> 01:00:40.540 align:middle line:84%
which is A. Then agent's action,
the change of agent's action,

01:00:40.540 --> 01:00:44.720 align:middle line:84%
dS, is influenced
by external force,

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:48.910 align:middle line:84%
as well as the feedback
loop of the observation

01:00:48.910 --> 01:00:51.220 align:middle line:90%
plus some random noise.

01:00:51.220 --> 01:00:57.490 align:middle line:84%
So that's the basic
framework of crowd modeling.

01:00:57.490 --> 01:01:02.630 align:middle line:84%
When the agents behave
in normal conditions,

01:01:02.630 --> 01:01:04.190 align:middle line:90%
they are more rational.

01:01:04.190 --> 01:01:08.530 align:middle line:84%
So they are less reactive
to the observations.

01:01:08.530 --> 01:01:10.470 align:middle line:84%
Or you can call it
the feedback loop.

01:01:10.470 --> 01:01:13.900 align:middle line:84%
Parameter B is actually
taking a lower value.

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:20.230 align:middle line:84%
But when observation changes
in a more volatile fashion,

01:01:20.230 --> 01:01:23.230 align:middle line:90%
the agents tend to react more.

01:01:23.230 --> 01:01:26.100 align:middle line:90%
They get into a reactive state.

01:01:26.100 --> 01:01:31.410 align:middle line:84%
So more agents in the crowd
get into reactive state.

01:01:31.410 --> 01:01:35.970 align:middle line:84%
The crowd actually
becomes more synchronized.

01:01:35.970 --> 01:01:40.110 align:middle line:84%
So if we look at the
order parameter, which

01:01:40.110 --> 01:01:46.890 align:middle line:84%
I defined as the ratio of the
sum of all the actions together

01:01:46.890 --> 01:01:51.990 align:middle line:84%
divided by the sum of the
absolute number of the actions,

01:01:51.990 --> 01:01:56.860 align:middle line:84%
if they are all synchronized,
that number should be 1.

01:01:56.860 --> 01:02:00.180 align:middle line:84%
If they are not
synchronized at all,

01:02:00.180 --> 01:02:03.170 align:middle line:84%
that number can be
very close to 0.

01:02:03.170 --> 01:02:06.170 align:middle line:84%
So what the first
chart on the left shows

01:02:06.170 --> 01:02:10.100 align:middle line:84%
is the order parameter
as a function

01:02:10.100 --> 01:02:15.170 align:middle line:84%
of number of agents in the
crowd in the reactive state.

01:02:15.170 --> 01:02:21.110 align:middle line:84%
So then when more agents become
reactive to the observation,

01:02:21.110 --> 01:02:25.730 align:middle line:84%
the order parameter
can become very high.

01:02:25.730 --> 01:02:28.640 align:middle line:90%
The crowd gets synchronized.

01:02:28.640 --> 01:02:33.920 align:middle line:84%
And on the right, you can see
the order parameter is also

01:02:33.920 --> 01:02:39.330 align:middle line:84%
a function of the random noise
term in the previous page.

01:02:39.330 --> 01:02:44.390 align:middle line:84%
The higher the noise, the
less likely the agents--

01:02:44.390 --> 01:02:46.490 align:middle line:90%
the crowd will be synchronized.

01:02:46.490 --> 01:02:51.170 align:middle line:84%
So this type of behavior, by
the way, is not new in physics.

01:02:51.170 --> 01:02:54.060 align:middle line:90%


01:02:54.060 --> 01:02:58.710 align:middle line:84%
In some of the circuit
engineering systems,

01:02:58.710 --> 01:03:01.150 align:middle line:84%
when you inject a
noise, you can also

01:03:01.150 --> 01:03:04.070 align:middle line:84%
reduce the order of
parameter synchronization.

01:03:04.070 --> 01:03:07.960 align:middle line:84%
But in social
science, this actually

01:03:07.960 --> 01:03:12.040 align:middle line:90%
is also a similar behavior.

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:16.910 align:middle line:84%
So I further simulated
the bubble b process.

01:03:16.910 --> 01:03:21.040 align:middle line:84%
So when you have an external
force showing on the upper left,

01:03:21.040 --> 01:03:27.670 align:middle line:84%
it's kind of ramping up, then
coming down, then oscillate.

01:03:27.670 --> 01:03:31.930 align:middle line:84%
The number of agents
in reactive state

01:03:31.930 --> 01:03:35.500 align:middle line:84%
starts to peak up,
then also drop when

01:03:35.500 --> 01:03:39.370 align:middle line:90%
the external shock diminishes.

01:03:39.370 --> 01:03:42.850 align:middle line:84%
The observation,
as a result, also

01:03:42.850 --> 01:03:46.520 align:middle line:90%
follows, but then comes down.

01:03:46.520 --> 01:03:50.000 align:middle line:84%
But if the external
shock, on the other hand,

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:54.190 align:middle line:84%
maintains at a
higher level forever,

01:03:54.190 --> 01:04:00.750 align:middle line:84%
eventually, all the members
of the crowd, all the agents,

01:04:00.750 --> 01:04:02.820 align:middle line:90%
get into the reactive state.

01:04:02.820 --> 01:04:06.730 align:middle line:84%
So the whole crowd
becomes synchronized.

01:04:06.730 --> 01:04:11.290 align:middle line:84%
The observation basically
shoots through the roof.

01:04:11.290 --> 01:04:14.650 align:middle line:84%
So it becomes a very
unstable system.

01:04:14.650 --> 01:04:16.230 align:middle line:90%
So that's the bubble.

01:04:16.230 --> 01:04:18.700 align:middle line:84%
At that point, when the
system becomes unstable,

01:04:18.700 --> 01:04:19.745 align:middle line:90%
your bubble will burst.

01:04:19.745 --> 01:04:24.210 align:middle line:90%


01:04:24.210 --> 01:04:28.000 align:middle line:84%
So that's the part of
the crowding behavior.

01:04:28.000 --> 01:04:32.730 align:middle line:84%
So the purpose of showing that
is, when we look at portfolio

01:04:32.730 --> 01:04:37.680 align:middle line:84%
management, this type of bubble
forming and bursting process

01:04:37.680 --> 01:04:42.450 align:middle line:84%
is a very important aspect
of our understanding

01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:45.250 align:middle line:90%
beyond the fundamental analysis.

01:04:45.250 --> 01:04:49.533 align:middle line:84%
This is all about
behavioral finance.

01:04:49.533 --> 01:04:52.800 align:middle line:90%


01:04:52.800 --> 01:04:55.710 align:middle line:84%
It has nothing to do with the
fundamental of the economy

01:04:55.710 --> 01:04:58.320 align:middle line:90%
or the fundamental of a company.

01:04:58.320 --> 01:05:03.660 align:middle line:84%
But then you probably also have
heard power law distribution.

01:05:03.660 --> 01:05:05.820 align:middle line:84%
So what is power
law distribution?

01:05:05.820 --> 01:05:08.910 align:middle line:84%
It's the winner take
all, the 80-20 rule,

01:05:08.910 --> 01:05:13.320 align:middle line:84%
or the Matthews effect, many
different terms describing

01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:21.390 align:middle line:84%
essentially a social phenomenon
that there are a small number

01:05:21.390 --> 01:05:26.730 align:middle line:84%
or percentage of the agents
taking a majority share

01:05:26.730 --> 01:05:30.070 align:middle line:90%
of something in the group.

01:05:30.070 --> 01:05:33.220 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about
wealth distribution.

01:05:33.220 --> 01:05:38.110 align:middle line:84%
So 20% of the people probably
have 80% of the wealth.

01:05:38.110 --> 01:05:41.560 align:middle line:90%
And this is also true.

01:05:41.560 --> 01:05:44.160 align:middle line:90%
You look at the top 20%.

01:05:44.160 --> 01:05:49.180 align:middle line:84%
Within that 20%, the top
20% has 80% of the wealth.

01:05:49.180 --> 01:05:51.940 align:middle line:84%
Then within that,
it's still true.

01:05:51.940 --> 01:05:56.390 align:middle line:84%
This is so-called scale
free or scale invariance.

01:05:56.390 --> 01:06:01.110 align:middle line:84%
This is the unique property
of power law distribution.

01:06:01.110 --> 01:06:07.910 align:middle line:84%
So in mathematical terms, the
probability of b times x-- x

01:06:07.910 --> 01:06:09.240 align:middle line:90%
is the random variable.

01:06:09.240 --> 01:06:11.150 align:middle line:90%
b is the scaling factor--

01:06:11.150 --> 01:06:17.000 align:middle line:84%
equal to some function of g of
b times the probability of x.

01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:20.390 align:middle line:84%
So this you can
prove mathematically.

01:06:20.390 --> 01:06:25.970 align:middle line:84%
If and only if this p of x
is a power law distribution,

01:06:25.970 --> 01:06:30.170 align:middle line:84%
that scale-free
property maintains.

01:06:30.170 --> 01:06:33.410 align:middle line:84%
So an example of power
law distribution,

01:06:33.410 --> 01:06:35.900 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned the
wealth distribution.

01:06:35.900 --> 01:06:38.490 align:middle line:90%
Venture capital funds returns--

01:06:38.490 --> 01:06:44.300 align:middle line:84%
the top tier funds tend to
get the majority of the profit

01:06:44.300 --> 01:06:46.310 align:middle line:90%
of the startup companies.

01:06:46.310 --> 01:06:49.020 align:middle line:84%
There's a book
published two years ago.

01:06:49.020 --> 01:06:55.640 align:middle line:84%
It's called Power Law,
by Sebastian Mallaby.

01:06:55.640 --> 01:06:57.020 align:middle line:90%
You should check it out.

01:06:57.020 --> 01:06:58.160 align:middle line:90%
It's very interesting.

01:06:58.160 --> 01:06:59.720 align:middle line:90%
It's not mathematical.

01:06:59.720 --> 01:07:04.600 align:middle line:84%
But it talks about how
the top tier VC firms

01:07:04.600 --> 01:07:07.840 align:middle line:90%
winner take all phenomenon.

01:07:07.840 --> 01:07:10.630 align:middle line:90%
City sizes, same thing.

01:07:10.630 --> 01:07:12.700 align:middle line:90%
Big cities get bigger.

01:07:12.700 --> 01:07:13.510 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:07:13.510 --> 01:07:17.350 align:middle line:84%
In the network theory,
because all the nodes

01:07:17.350 --> 01:07:20.080 align:middle line:84%
want to connect to
a more popular node

01:07:20.080 --> 01:07:22.090 align:middle line:90%
to attract more traffic.

01:07:22.090 --> 01:07:27.760 align:middle line:84%
So the network space, internet
connections, web pages

01:07:27.760 --> 01:07:33.610 align:middle line:84%
or social media posts, this type
of power law is very common.

01:07:33.610 --> 01:07:40.700 align:middle line:84%
But in nature, we've observed
a lot of normal distributions,

01:07:40.700 --> 01:07:42.940 align:middle line:90%
such as height.

01:07:42.940 --> 01:07:46.030 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of people are
probably centered around

01:07:46.030 --> 01:07:55.350 align:middle line:84%
call it between 5 to around 6
feet, maybe just under 6 feet.

01:07:55.350 --> 01:07:59.520 align:middle line:84%
This is the majority
of the population.

01:07:59.520 --> 01:08:02.110 align:middle line:84%
It's not like most
people are very short,

01:08:02.110 --> 01:08:06.030 align:middle line:84%
but there are a few giants,
really, just much taller.

01:08:06.030 --> 01:08:07.150 align:middle line:90%
So that's power law.

01:08:07.150 --> 01:08:12.450 align:middle line:84%
Power law does not exist
in the height distribution.

01:08:12.450 --> 01:08:17.130 align:middle line:84%
So now, we commented on many
social phenomena obey power law

01:08:17.130 --> 01:08:17.890 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

01:08:17.890 --> 01:08:19.109 align:middle line:90%
But why?

01:08:19.109 --> 01:08:24.870 align:middle line:84%
This is the question that
always makes me very curious.

01:08:24.870 --> 01:08:32.490 align:middle line:84%
So what's the cause of such
skewed wealth distribution?

01:08:32.490 --> 01:08:36.029 align:middle line:84%
So eventually, I
realized this actually

01:08:36.029 --> 01:08:38.800 align:middle line:84%
has a lot to do with
the crowd interaction.

01:08:38.800 --> 01:08:45.330 align:middle line:84%
So the same mechanism we
looked at earlier, the agents

01:08:45.330 --> 01:08:47.200 align:middle line:90%
have the feedback loop.

01:08:47.200 --> 01:08:51.529 align:middle line:84%
That's the reason of the cause
of this type of power law.

01:08:51.529 --> 01:08:56.300 align:middle line:84%
In nature, you don't have that
kind of a feedback mechanism.

01:08:56.300 --> 01:08:59.040 align:middle line:84%
Once you are born, you
grow to a certain height.

01:08:59.040 --> 01:09:01.130 align:middle line:90%
Your height doesn't change.

01:09:01.130 --> 01:09:07.490 align:middle line:84%
But in social phenomena,
that keeps changing.

01:09:07.490 --> 01:09:10.370 align:middle line:84%
So agents have different
powers influencing

01:09:10.370 --> 01:09:13.460 align:middle line:90%
the market in financial market.

01:09:13.460 --> 01:09:15.710 align:middle line:90%
The rich can get richer.

01:09:15.710 --> 01:09:18.960 align:middle line:84%
So we need to model
each agent differently.

01:09:18.960 --> 01:09:21.700 align:middle line:84%
So we call it
heterogeneous agents.

01:09:21.700 --> 01:09:24.770 align:middle line:90%


01:09:24.770 --> 01:09:29.600 align:middle line:84%
So for a random variable
x, the normal distribution

01:09:29.600 --> 01:09:34.040 align:middle line:84%
we're all familiar with is
the e to the x minus the mean

01:09:34.040 --> 01:09:36.380 align:middle line:90%
divided by 2 sigma squared.

01:09:36.380 --> 01:09:39.979 align:middle line:84%
But for power law,
people, a lot of times,

01:09:39.979 --> 01:09:43.010 align:middle line:84%
get confused about
the terminologies.

01:09:43.010 --> 01:09:49.250 align:middle line:84%
The Pareto law talks about
the cumulative density

01:09:49.250 --> 01:09:52.970 align:middle line:90%
function of the probability.

01:09:52.970 --> 01:10:02.610 align:middle line:84%
And Zipf's law talks about
the rank of that outcome.

01:10:02.610 --> 01:10:04.820 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the
random variable x

01:10:04.820 --> 01:10:11.810 align:middle line:84%
versus the rank of X, which is
more displayed in this chart.

01:10:11.810 --> 01:10:13.730 align:middle line:90%
But they are all related.

01:10:13.730 --> 01:10:18.080 align:middle line:84%
When we normally look at the
power law in a probability

01:10:18.080 --> 01:10:23.090 align:middle line:84%
density function,
that behaves like this

01:10:23.090 --> 01:10:25.260 align:middle line:90%
versus the normal distribution.

01:10:25.260 --> 01:10:30.530 align:middle line:84%
So even distribution, of
course, is a delta function.

01:10:30.530 --> 01:10:34.670 align:middle line:84%
So the parameters of
these three different ways

01:10:34.670 --> 01:10:39.900 align:middle line:84%
of representing power law, they
are all linked by that formula.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:43.130 align:middle line:84%
So power law probability
density function

01:10:43.130 --> 01:10:46.652 align:middle line:90%
is C over x to the power of a.

01:10:46.652 --> 01:10:53.270 align:middle line:84%
a is related to the cumulative
density function, power k,

01:10:53.270 --> 01:10:58.430 align:middle line:84%
as well as the Zipf's
law of parameter b.

01:10:58.430 --> 01:11:01.060 align:middle line:90%


01:11:01.060 --> 01:11:05.290 align:middle line:84%
So if we look at the
feedback mechanism,

01:11:05.290 --> 01:11:13.540 align:middle line:84%
we can derive that the agents
with more power, in this case,

01:11:13.540 --> 01:11:18.020 align:middle line:84%
which is represented by
a, varying over time,

01:11:18.020 --> 01:11:22.420 align:middle line:84%
can gain more power when they
bet in the right direction,

01:11:22.420 --> 01:11:24.700 align:middle line:84%
when the observation
shows their betting is

01:11:24.700 --> 01:11:27.160 align:middle line:90%
in the right direction.

01:11:27.160 --> 01:11:28.730 align:middle line:90%
So that's how rich get richer.

01:11:28.730 --> 01:11:33.670 align:middle line:84%
So that's how concentration
of power is formed.

01:11:33.670 --> 01:11:37.635 align:middle line:84%
Super agents will be
born, in this case.

01:11:37.635 --> 01:11:42.190 align:middle line:84%
The system may or may not
become stable when that happens.

01:11:42.190 --> 01:11:45.270 align:middle line:84%
And I mean, this
actually can be expanded

01:11:45.270 --> 01:11:47.710 align:middle line:90%
into a discussion of entropy.

01:11:47.710 --> 01:11:49.690 align:middle line:90%
What is order?

01:11:49.690 --> 01:11:51.250 align:middle line:90%
What is stable?

01:11:51.250 --> 01:11:55.620 align:middle line:84%
But this is beyond the
scope of this lecture.

01:11:55.620 --> 01:11:59.880 align:middle line:84%
Just to come back, this is
all relevant to portfolio

01:11:59.880 --> 01:12:01.350 align:middle line:90%
management.

01:12:01.350 --> 01:12:07.710 align:middle line:84%
So I want to summarize, in this
page, of what we learned today.

01:12:07.710 --> 01:12:12.330 align:middle line:84%
So portfolio management is about
how to size each investment.

01:12:12.330 --> 01:12:15.100 align:middle line:84%
You need to clarify
your objectives.

01:12:15.100 --> 01:12:18.270 align:middle line:84%
You need to know
your loss tolerance.

01:12:18.270 --> 01:12:19.920 align:middle line:90%
Diversification helps.

01:12:19.920 --> 01:12:22.710 align:middle line:90%
But you need to rebalance.

01:12:22.710 --> 01:12:24.550 align:middle line:90%
Volatility is not risk.

01:12:24.550 --> 01:12:28.540 align:middle line:84%
Sharpe ratio doesn't capture
skill nor provide sizing.

01:12:28.540 --> 01:12:32.880 align:middle line:84%
We should use the new
ratio I presented to you.

01:12:32.880 --> 01:12:35.790 align:middle line:84%
Capital market assumptions
are not reliable.

01:12:35.790 --> 01:12:40.140 align:middle line:84%
And the crowding behavior
can push markets to extremes.

01:12:40.140 --> 01:12:44.060 align:middle line:84%
You should pay attention
to powerful super agents.

01:12:44.060 --> 01:12:49.730 align:middle line:84%
Who are the most powerful super
agents you can think about?

01:12:49.730 --> 01:12:52.500 align:middle line:84%
Anybody has any
idea in the market?

01:12:52.500 --> 01:12:54.320 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: The Fed.

01:12:54.320 --> 01:12:56.030 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yes, the Fed.

01:12:56.030 --> 01:12:56.660 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:12:56.660 --> 01:12:58.827 align:middle line:84%
So when you invest
in the market,

01:12:58.827 --> 01:13:00.410 align:middle line:84%
you have to pay
attention because they

01:13:00.410 --> 01:13:06.140 align:middle line:84%
have such a big power to change
the direction of markets.

01:13:06.140 --> 01:13:09.120 align:middle line:84%
And in the old days, some
hedge funds have more power.

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:12.560 align:middle line:84%
But I would say, nowadays, it's
definitely the governments.

01:13:12.560 --> 01:13:17.750 align:middle line:84%
So you need to understand the
key drivers of your portfolio.

01:13:17.750 --> 01:13:21.110 align:middle line:84%
So I want to stop
the lecture here.

01:13:21.110 --> 01:13:23.010 align:middle line:90%
I want to take some questions.

01:13:23.010 --> 01:13:25.790 align:middle line:84%
Then I will save a
couple of minutes

01:13:25.790 --> 01:13:27.480 align:middle line:84%
to talk about the
investment game.

01:13:27.480 --> 01:13:33.080 align:middle line:84%
But before I get there,
let's open up for questions.

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:35.370 align:middle line:84%
I packed in a lot of
content for today.

01:13:35.370 --> 01:13:37.220 align:middle line:90%
I know.

01:13:37.220 --> 01:13:42.220 align:middle line:84%
The idea is not
necessary to ask you

01:13:42.220 --> 01:13:45.160 align:middle line:84%
to follow in
details because some

01:13:45.160 --> 01:13:47.440 align:middle line:84%
of the math you probably
need to derive it yourself

01:13:47.440 --> 01:13:48.710 align:middle line:90%
to get a feel.

01:13:48.710 --> 01:13:52.330 align:middle line:84%
But I want to give you the
concepts of what the portfolio

01:13:52.330 --> 01:13:53.930 align:middle line:90%
construction is all about.

01:13:53.930 --> 01:13:56.570 align:middle line:84%
It's about sizing
of investments.

01:13:56.570 --> 01:14:00.320 align:middle line:84%
Sizing is about comparison
about investments.

01:14:00.320 --> 01:14:06.430 align:middle line:84%
So how to compare them, that's
what it really comes down to.

01:14:06.430 --> 01:14:12.370 align:middle line:90%
So for that, please, questions.

01:14:12.370 --> 01:14:13.570 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:14:13.570 --> 01:14:17.650 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
that can influence

01:14:17.650 --> 01:14:21.880 align:middle line:84%
investments is taxes that you
have to pay on capital gains

01:14:21.880 --> 01:14:24.640 align:middle line:84%
or how long you own
something can vary

01:14:24.640 --> 01:14:27.880 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] certain
types of investments

01:14:27.880 --> 01:14:31.190 align:middle line:84%
might be more tax
sensitive than others.

01:14:31.190 --> 01:14:35.890 align:middle line:84%
Is that something that
is considered heavily

01:14:35.890 --> 01:14:39.670 align:middle line:84%
in terms of investment
supports [INAUDIBLE]?

01:14:39.670 --> 01:14:43.350 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: Yeah, so
that's a great question.

01:14:43.350 --> 01:14:45.800 align:middle line:84%
Endowments are tax
free, by the way.

01:14:45.800 --> 01:14:46.910 align:middle line:90%
So don't consider that.

01:14:46.910 --> 01:14:49.090 align:middle line:84%
But if you run a
family office, you

01:14:49.090 --> 01:14:54.230 align:middle line:84%
have to be very careful to
plan your capital gains.

01:14:54.230 --> 01:14:57.520 align:middle line:84%
So the family offices,
or private investors,

01:14:57.520 --> 01:15:00.790 align:middle line:84%
tend to invest more in
long term private equity

01:15:00.790 --> 01:15:03.050 align:middle line:84%
because they don't have
to realize those gains.

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:07.070 align:middle line:84%
They invest less in public
equity for the tax reason.

01:15:07.070 --> 01:15:10.240 align:middle line:84%
So it is a very
important parameter.

01:15:10.240 --> 01:15:12.880 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:15:12.880 --> 01:15:14.000 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:15:14.000 --> 01:15:14.990 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:15:14.990 --> 01:15:17.920 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What is the
best risk measure?

01:15:17.920 --> 01:15:19.510 align:middle line:84%
What should you
measuree risk with?

01:15:19.510 --> 01:15:22.300 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: So I still
think we should

01:15:22.300 --> 01:15:27.100 align:middle line:84%
try to understand the potential
loss or expected loss--

01:15:27.100 --> 01:15:31.390 align:middle line:84%
so that's the L I
discussed earlier--

01:15:31.390 --> 01:15:38.200 align:middle line:84%
because people tend to think
gain and loss are symmetrical.

01:15:38.200 --> 01:15:41.190 align:middle line:84%
But normally, it's not
the case because you

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:45.610 align:middle line:84%
can construct your positions
of very asymmetrical payouts.

01:15:45.610 --> 01:15:49.020 align:middle line:84%
So you need to understand, when
you buy an option, of course,

01:15:49.020 --> 01:15:52.810 align:middle line:84%
your maximum loss is the
option premium you paid.

01:15:52.810 --> 01:15:54.100 align:middle line:90%
It's very clear.

01:15:54.100 --> 01:15:58.060 align:middle line:84%
But when you buy a stock,
that may not be so clear.

01:15:58.060 --> 01:16:01.020 align:middle line:84%
So you need to understand
how much the stock can lose.

01:16:01.020 --> 01:16:02.310 align:middle line:90%
It can move down.

01:16:02.310 --> 01:16:05.850 align:middle line:84%
And what's the likely
range it can move down

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:08.290 align:middle line:90%
to and those kind of things.

01:16:08.290 --> 01:16:12.810 align:middle line:84%
So I think the risk measurement
should be shifted or focused

01:16:12.810 --> 01:16:15.040 align:middle line:90%
more on the downside protection.

01:16:15.040 --> 01:16:19.924 align:middle line:84%
Understand your downside,
which is really expected loss.

01:16:19.924 --> 01:16:22.170 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]
the question expected

01:16:22.170 --> 01:16:24.900 align:middle line:90%
loss versus worst-case loss.

01:16:24.900 --> 01:16:26.220 align:middle line:90%
How do you--

01:16:26.220 --> 01:16:28.570 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: Yeah, those
are two different things.

01:16:28.570 --> 01:16:32.190 align:middle line:84%
So roughly, you can think
about worst loss may be

01:16:32.190 --> 01:16:35.160 align:middle line:90%
a few standard deviations away.

01:16:35.160 --> 01:16:38.870 align:middle line:84%
I mean, expected loss
probably is really

01:16:38.870 --> 01:16:43.010 align:middle line:84%
the weighted average
of outcome times

01:16:43.010 --> 01:16:45.860 align:middle line:90%
the payout, that kind of thing.

01:16:45.860 --> 01:16:51.350 align:middle line:84%
So you cannot construct your
portfolio all based on worst

01:16:51.350 --> 01:16:51.960 align:middle line:90%
case.

01:16:51.960 --> 01:16:53.970 align:middle line:84%
Then you will not
be doing anything.

01:16:53.970 --> 01:16:58.610 align:middle line:84%
So that's why expected loss
is probably a better way

01:16:58.610 --> 01:17:02.640 align:middle line:84%
to measure for sizing
your investments.

01:17:02.640 --> 01:17:05.750 align:middle line:84%
But you need to be aware
of your worst loss, yeah,

01:17:05.750 --> 01:17:07.560 align:middle line:90%
in case something happens.

01:17:07.560 --> 01:17:08.060 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:17:08.060 --> 01:17:08.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:17:08.720 --> 01:17:10.303 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I was going
to say, going off

01:17:10.303 --> 01:17:13.080 align:middle line:84%
the worst-case loss, [INAUDIBLE]
implement stop losses?

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:15.370 align:middle line:84%
How effective have
you found those?

01:17:15.370 --> 01:17:18.020 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] as well?

01:17:18.020 --> 01:17:18.830 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yeah.

01:17:18.830 --> 01:17:23.510 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, I think it's a very
good question both of you

01:17:23.510 --> 01:17:25.250 align:middle line:90%
brought up.

01:17:25.250 --> 01:17:27.930 align:middle line:84%
Stop loss is an
interesting concept.

01:17:27.930 --> 01:17:32.160 align:middle line:84%
So stop loss typically is
not set at the worst loss.

01:17:32.160 --> 01:17:36.820 align:middle line:84%
It's set usually quite
close, probably even closer

01:17:36.820 --> 01:17:39.430 align:middle line:90%
than the expected loss.

01:17:39.430 --> 01:17:42.710 align:middle line:84%
If you're trading at the
hedge fund platforms,

01:17:42.710 --> 01:17:46.330 align:middle line:84%
they give you a very tight
stop loss for the reason

01:17:46.330 --> 01:17:50.586 align:middle line:84%
that they try to
artificially construct

01:17:50.586 --> 01:17:53.500 align:middle line:84%
an asymmetric payout
for the portfolio

01:17:53.500 --> 01:17:55.190 align:middle line:90%
managers or the traders.

01:17:55.190 --> 01:17:58.400 align:middle line:84%
When you lose a little bit,
they cut your position in half.

01:17:58.400 --> 01:18:00.980 align:middle line:84%
Then you lose another
few percentage points,

01:18:00.980 --> 01:18:02.780 align:middle line:84%
cut another half,
then get you out.

01:18:02.780 --> 01:18:07.780 align:middle line:84%
So when you make money, they
try to let you ride on the winds

01:18:07.780 --> 01:18:11.600 align:middle line:84%
to create that kind of
asymmetric optionality.

01:18:11.600 --> 01:18:16.030 align:middle line:84%
But that forces you
to pick investment

01:18:16.030 --> 01:18:19.820 align:middle line:84%
that has that kind of a
probability distribution.

01:18:19.820 --> 01:18:23.560 align:middle line:84%
So if you think an investment
has a worst case loss which

01:18:23.560 --> 01:18:28.640 align:middle line:84%
is very bad, but has a
very low probability,

01:18:28.640 --> 01:18:34.020 align:middle line:84%
let's just say, you need
to think about if that--

01:18:34.020 --> 01:18:37.260 align:middle line:84%
almost like the firm
takes that risk for you

01:18:37.260 --> 01:18:40.900 align:middle line:84%
because they are
responsible to cut you out.

01:18:40.900 --> 01:18:43.840 align:middle line:84%
But if you think the
upside is very big,

01:18:43.840 --> 01:18:46.650 align:middle line:90%
then you should take that bet.

01:18:46.650 --> 01:18:48.330 align:middle line:90%
You get it.

01:18:48.330 --> 01:18:49.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:18:49.290 --> 01:18:51.550 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:18:51.550 --> 01:18:54.600 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Yeah, right.

01:18:54.600 --> 01:19:01.000 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's a lot of the
issues with hedge funds.

01:19:01.000 --> 01:19:05.340 align:middle line:84%
They get a cut on the profit,
but they don't bear the downside

01:19:05.340 --> 01:19:06.870 align:middle line:90%
responsibilities.

01:19:06.870 --> 01:19:11.250 align:middle line:84%
So they are long a
free call option.

01:19:11.250 --> 01:19:11.837 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:19:11.837 --> 01:19:14.420 align:middle line:84%
So that's why a lot of people
want to be a hedge fund manager.

01:19:14.420 --> 01:19:17.280 align:middle line:90%


01:19:17.280 --> 01:19:20.740 align:middle line:84%
So I will just touch
upon the investment game.

01:19:20.740 --> 01:19:24.300 align:middle line:90%
We already talked about it.

01:19:24.300 --> 01:19:29.220 align:middle line:84%
When we pass November
15, you should

01:19:29.220 --> 01:19:32.680 align:middle line:84%
keep track of G and
L numbers and let

01:19:32.680 --> 01:19:34.460 align:middle line:90%
us know how you performed.

01:19:34.460 --> 01:19:36.980 align:middle line:90%
And I will try to verify.

01:19:36.980 --> 01:19:40.100 align:middle line:84%
But last slide, I want to
just coming back to the point,

01:19:40.100 --> 01:19:42.960 align:middle line:84%
a lot of people ask how to
become a hedge fund manager.

01:19:42.960 --> 01:19:45.760 align:middle line:90%


01:19:45.760 --> 01:19:50.470 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I want to poll the
audience here a little bit.

01:19:50.470 --> 01:19:54.640 align:middle line:84%
Is there a value, you think,
from that trading or investment

01:19:54.640 --> 01:19:57.530 align:middle line:84%
game to do some
paper trading games?

01:19:57.530 --> 01:20:01.690 align:middle line:84%
Or do you think you want
more lectures on the research

01:20:01.690 --> 01:20:05.860 align:middle line:84%
process, how to do data
collection, signal generation,

01:20:05.860 --> 01:20:09.850 align:middle line:84%
backtesting, and all the things
and the portfolio optimization,

01:20:09.850 --> 01:20:11.320 align:middle line:90%
as we talk about today?

01:20:11.320 --> 01:20:16.630 align:middle line:84%
Or some people say, I just
want to learn more about how

01:20:16.630 --> 01:20:19.220 align:middle line:84%
to become a manager,
how to do fundraising,

01:20:19.220 --> 01:20:22.360 align:middle line:84%
how to build a team, how
to negotiate the terms

01:20:22.360 --> 01:20:26.570 align:middle line:84%
and how to assemble
systems and a back office,

01:20:26.570 --> 01:20:29.420 align:middle line:90%
legal taxes, and so on.

01:20:29.420 --> 01:20:35.750 align:middle line:84%
So I'm actually thinking
maybe there's something we,

01:20:35.750 --> 01:20:40.430 align:middle line:84%
as an organization, or some
other people should really just

01:20:40.430 --> 01:20:45.860 align:middle line:84%
help the younger potential
PMs a little more

01:20:45.860 --> 01:20:50.570 align:middle line:84%
to incubate them to become
a better fund manager.

01:20:50.570 --> 01:20:55.830 align:middle line:84%
If any one of you think that's
something valid, this idea,

01:20:55.830 --> 01:21:01.760 align:middle line:84%
you can talk to me, email me,
or just come to me after class.

01:21:01.760 --> 01:21:05.150 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's
something beyond what

01:21:05.150 --> 01:21:11.360 align:middle line:84%
we talk in this class that could
be useful to any one of you.

01:21:11.360 --> 01:21:15.525 align:middle line:84%
So with that, thank
you very much.

01:21:15.525 --> 01:21:28.000 align:middle line:90%